ist of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "housing arts and civil rights committee", "dionne foster", "eddie lin", "debora juarez", "maritza rivera", "joy hollingsworth", "alexis mercedes rinck", "amy nguyen", "office of arts and culture", "seattle", "city hall", "council chamber", "600 4th avenue", "seattle arts", "appt 03471", "appt 03487", "tera beach"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778632810.36656, "duration_sec": 1.6368582248687744, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, so are you all familiar with the way that Mercury is doing state management and stuff? Nope. Okay, so I think right now I'm feeling like somewhere between Redux and Mercury. I think that, and just to explain what Mercury does, it's very similar to Redux in some ways, obviously with different nouns, because you have to have different nouns to explain the same concepts. But yeah, so I think there's like..."], [0, "And right now is, I don't know, right now is like a renaissance for front-end stuff. Like, state management has always been, like, one of the things nobody ever really put a serious amount of thought into. And right now there's, like, ten things out with completely different ways of doing state management, and they're all pretty good. So..."], [0, "I can't really predict the future of what I think it's going to be, but my gut feeling is somewhere between Redux and Mercury. Have you seen Victor Safkin's experimentation with observables in a Redux implementation? No, I haven't."], [0, "That was a very interesting twist on Redux, where using observables cut out a lot of, I don't know if boilerplate is the right word, but cut a lot of interesting pieces out and added a bunch of interesting pieces in. Boilerplate is always the right word, Joe. But I think it's probably somewhere closer to cycle, right? What he did was similar, like halfway between Redux and cycle, as I understand cycle. Yeah, you should link me to that. That sounds interesting. I will."], [0, "Yeah, it's definitely cool to look at. I don't know if I would recommend people build stuff with it. They don't even have a logo. I wouldn't touch it with a 10-mile pole. I know, right? Yeah, but it has more than 20 stars. Logos are really easy to make. So if anybody is doing open source stuff and they want to know how to make a good logo, I'll give you a secret. Go to the Squarespace Logo Maker website."], [0, "All of the logos that I make now are Squarespace logos. That's amazing. Yeah, so it seems like it would be really cheesy, but they actually come out looking kind of nice. They have nice fonts and the logos. So you type in a word, and they give you little clip art kind of things, and you click it, and it goes in, except all the clip art is really cool, and all the fonts are nice. I always go to Fiverr. That's all the investment I'm willing to make sometimes. Yeah. This has changed my life."], [0, "Yeah. We should chalk that up to a pick. Are we ready for picks? I think so. It just seemed like we're winding down. All right. Squarespace Logo Maker has a"], [0, "like a deer icon with antlers, just so you know. It's cool looking. Joe, what are your picks? Well, I'm going to pick Victor Savkin's post. It was actually written about managing state in an Angular application, but he basically adapted the ideas of Redux, but utilized RxJS, which Angular is heavily into, and did some really cool stuff, and I've heard a lot of good feedback about it from other people that are heavily into the React world and Redux world, saying that these are some really good ideas. So that'll be my first pick."], [0, "is Victor Sapkin's post. I think the title of it is Managing State in Angular 2 Applications if you want to Google it and not find the show notes. And then for my second pick, I really wish I could pick 10 Cloverfield Lane. I wanted to see it this last weekend and didn't see it. So instead I'm going to pick Laser Team. I have no idea if it's going to be any good or not. It's kind of like a campy sort of parody su