{"timestamp": 1778634411.6068835, "duration_sec": 4.060667037963867, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "webpage_metadata_generation", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "webpage_metadata_generation", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "webpage_markdown", "data": {"window": [[0, "Not too long ago, Ipswich conservationists were nervously eyeing Scott Farm and hoping that a plan to put 39 new homes on part of it wouldn\u2019t pan out.\n\nToday, most of the farmland is permanently preserved as open space, and the rest is protected from full-scale development with strict land-use rules.\n\nHad it not been for a unique partnership between local environmentalists, a Boston-based developer and the town, the Scott Farm \u2013 with its scenic views and frontage along Ipswich River \u2013 could have easily been a distant memory. While a portion of the 83-acre farm can still be built into a cluster-style development of up to eight homes, local conservations view that as a much better alternative than the 39 single-family homes originally slated for the property.\n\nThis is one of those creative compromises and approaches that help stretch local dollars for open space protection, said Tracie Hines, Ipswich\u2019s open space program manager.\n\nWith skyrocketing real estate prices, North Shore communi"], [0, "Hotel Fixed and Variable Costs: Complete - Prostay\nAug 31, 2025 \u00b7 Learn how to manage hotel fixed and variable costs effectively. Discover strategies to control expenses, improve profitability, and optimize operations."], [1, "Hospitality Financial Leadership - Hotel Financial Coach\nJul 17, 2022 \u00b7 Now that you have all your costs for fixed , variable and percentage of revenue items laid out you can calculate the breakeven. For this example, I am using a 110-room hotel . I build my summary to pull together the revenues and costs . All I need to do is play with the numbers in the two yellow cells to find the break even."], [2, "Hotel & Hospitality Feasibility Study | ADR & Occupancy ...\nIndependent feasibility analysis for hotels , motels, and hospitality properties. STR-validated market projections , RevPAR and ADR modeling, demand segmentation analysis , franchise economics evaluation, and financial projections built for SBA, USDA, and conventional lender approval."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": null, "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["hotel management", "hospitality management", "hotel finance", "budgeting", "financial management", "cost control", "fixed costs", "variable costs", "cost analysis", "revenue management", "financial projections", "hotel development", "hotel feasibility", "occupancy rates", "room pricing", "room revenue", "market projections", "lender approval", "zoning regulations", "land use planning"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778635823.6248739, "duration_sec": 3.1898036003112793, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "That's a good question. But I do know that it's fisheye and it's relatively low resolution. So it's really not designed. It wasn't. It wasn't designed for drone monitor. You can hope that you can kind of scrape up and have something from it. Yeah. But. Why didn't they? Put it in today. Put it in today. Put it in today."], [0, "Every time I've heard Carpathie talk about the problem and talking about like software 2.0 and how the machine learning is gobbling up everything, I think this is absolutely the right strategy. I think that he didn't write Navigate on autopilot. I think somebody else did and kind of hacked it on top of that stuff. I think when Carpathie says, wait a second, why did we hand code this lane change policy with all these magic numbers? We're going to learn it from data. They'll fix it. They already know what to do there."], [0, "Well, that's Andre's job is to turn everything into a learning problem and collect a huge amount of data. The reality is though, not every problem can be turned into a learning problem in the short term. In the end, everything will be a learning problem. The reality is-"], [0, "like if you wanna build L5 vehicles today, it will likely involve no learning. And that's the reality is, so at which point does learning start, it's the crutch statement that LiDAR is a crutch. At which point will learning get up to part of human performance? It's over human performance on ImageNet,"], [0, "classification on driving is the question still. It is a question. I'll say this, I'm here to play for 10 years. I'm not here to try to, I'm here to play for 10 years and make money along the way. I'm not here to try to promise people that I'm going to have my L5 taxi network up and working in two years. Do you think that was a mistake? Yes."], [0, "What do you think was the motivation behind saying that? Other companies are also promising L5 vehicles with very different approaches in 2020, 2021, 2022."], [0, "If anybody would like to bet me that those things do not pan out, I will bet you. Even money? Even money? I'll bet you as much as you want. So are you worried about what's going to happen? Because you're not in full agreement on that. What's going to happen when 2022, 2021 come around and nobody has fleets of autonomous vehicles?"], [0, "Well, you can look at the history. If you go back five years ago, they were all promised by 2018 and 2017. But they weren't that strong of promises. I mean, Ford really declared pretty... I think not many have declared as definitively as they have now these dates."], [0, "Well, okay, so let's separate L4 and L5. Do I think that it's possible for Waymo to continue to kind of like hack on their system until it gets to level four in Chandler, Arizona? Yes. No safety driver? Chandler, Arizona? Yeah. By, sorry, which year are we talking about? Oh, I even think that's possible by like 2020, 2021. But level four Chandler, Arizona, not level five New York City."], [0, "Level four, meaning some very defined streets it works on really well. Very defined streets. And then practically these streets are pretty empty. If most of the streets are covered in Waymos, Waymo can kind of change the definition of what driving is, right? If your self-driving network is the majority of cars in an area, they only need to be safe with respect to each other and all the humans will need to learn to adapt to them."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["autonomous vehicles", "self driving", "machine learning", "software 20", "data collection", "autopilot", "lane changing", "hand coded rules", "lidar", "computer vision", "level 4", "level 5", "robotics", "transportation", "safety regulation", "forecasting", "business strategy", "competition"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778636317.5618656, "duration_sec": 5.118000268936157, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "March 11, 2026\nA regular meeting of the City Council of the City of Boston was held in the Christopher A. Iannella\nChamber, City Hall on Wednesday, March 11, 2026 at 12:11 PM.\nPresident Breadon in the Chair. Present Councilors Coletta Zapata, Culpepper, Durkan, FitzGerald,\nFlynn, Louijeune, Mejia, Murphy, Pep\u00e9n, Santana, Weber, and Worrell.\nCity Clerk Alex Geourntas delivered the invocation, and the meeting was opened with the Pledge of\nAllegiance.\nThe Chair moved to adopt the minutes from the March 4, 2026 City Council Meeting.\nMotion prevailed.\n_________________________________________________________________________\n0500 Message and order authorizing the City of Boston to accept and expend the\namount of Two Million Three Hundred Fifteen Thousand Nine Hundred\nSixty-Six Dollars And Eighty-Eight Cents ($2,315,966.88) in the form of a\ngrant, the FY26 State Elder Lunch Program Grant, awarded by MA\nExecutive Office of Elder Affairs to be administered by the Age Strong\nCommission. The gran"], [0, "RevPAR vs GOPPAR: Which Hotel Metric Actually Matters ...\nOct 18, 2025 \u00b7 Today, hotel administrators must evaluate numerous performance indicators to guarantee success. The focus is often on RevPAR , or \u201cRevenue Per Available Room,\u201d because it shows room profitability. Nevertheless, GOPPAR (Gross Operating Profit Per Available Room) is another vital metric."], [1, "RevPAR vs GOPPAR: Finding the Right Performance Metric for ...\nMar 3, 2025 \u00b7 The study found a high overall correlation (r=0.91) between RevPAR and GOPPAR across nearly 6,000 hotel /year observations. This suggests that for many properties, RevPAR remains a reasonable proxy ..."], [2, "Hotel KPIs: RevPAR to GOPPAR Explained [2026] - otelciro.com\nOct 27, 2025 \u00b7 RevPAR , TRevPAR, GOPPAR , ADR, and occupancy rate \u2014 a comprehensive guide to the critical revenue metrics and KPI dashboard every hotel needs to track."], [3, "18 Trends to Watch in Hotel Distribution: 2023 and beyond\nJanuary 8, 2023 - Covid has accelerated this trend, making it increasingly difficult for hotel commercial teams to understand the ever-changing travel and booking patterns and adjust pricing accordingly. This has led to a change in hotel product design and marketing as well. This is not only a challenge for hotels, but also for Travel Management Companies (TMCs) that serve their clients - the companies. While more and more companies are starting to address this type of mixed travel in their travel policies, a large part of it is still not managed. Although overall business travel has not yet returned to 2019 levels, the emergence of increasingly mixed formats has helped to support and revitalise this segment. However, as this category grows rapidly, hotels need to give travellers the tools they need to achieve a work-life mix similar to what they have at home when they are on the road."], [4, "HVS | HVS Global Hotel Industry: 2023 Recap and 2024 Outlook\nAs a result, we expect the nationwide hotel occupancy rate to reach pre-pandemic levels of 66% in 2024, with average rates anticipated to near INR 8,000 (32\u201334% above the pre-pandemic level) for the year . By Hok Yean Chee, President \u2013 Asia Pacific Overall hotel performance across the Asia ..."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["city of boston", "boston city council", "boston city hall", "christopher a. iannella chamber", "city clerk alex geourntas", "councilor coletta zapata", "councilor durkan", "councilor flynn", "councilor mejia", "councilor murphy", "councilor pep\u00e9n", "councilor santana", "councilor weber", "councilor worrell", "fy26 state elder lunch program grant", "massachusetts executive office of elder affairs", "age strong commission", "revpar", "goppar", "covid-19"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778637094.63373, "duration_sec": 5.11749005317688, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Seattle Council Committee Report\nGovernance and Utilities Committee\nCouncil Chamber, City Hall\nThursday, April 9, 2026, 9:30 AM\n600 4th Avenue\nSeattle, WA 98104\nThis meeting also constitutes a meeting of the City Council, provided that the meeting shall be conducted as a\ncommittee meeting under the Council Rules and Procedures, and Council action shall be limited to committee\nbusiness.\nPlease Note: Times listed are estimated\nA. Call To Order\nMeeting Start Time: 9:33 a.m.\nPresiding Officer: Council President Hollingsworth\nPresent: 4 - Joy Hollingsworth, Debora Juarez, Maritza Rivera,Dan Strauss\nExcused: 1 - Robert Kettle\nB. Approval of the Agenda\nBy unanimous consent, the Governance and Utilities Committee recessed\nfrom 9:36 a.m. to 9:41 a.m. The Governance and Utilities Committee came\nback to order at 9:43 a.m.\nPresent: 3 - Joy Hollingsworth, Debora Juarez,Dan Strauss\nExcused: 1 - Robert Kettle\nLate Arrival: 1 - Maritza Rivera\nC. Public Comment\nD. Items of Business\n1. Appt 03470 Appoin"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "governance and utilities committee", "joy hollingsworth", "debora juarez", "maritza rivera", "dan strauss", "robert kettle", "seattle city hall", "council chamber", "600 4th avenue seattle wa 98104", "council rules and procedures", "appt 03470"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778637264.7572465, "duration_sec": 2.043862819671631, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Welcome to Unsupervised Learning, a security AI and meaning-focused podcast that looks at how best to thrive as humans in a post-AI world. It combines original ideas, analysis, and mental models to bring not just the news, but why it matters and how to respond."], [0, "All right, welcome to Unsupervised Learning. This is Daniel Miesler. All right, I want to talk about writing fiction using AI today. And I want to show a couple of different things that I'm thinking about this, both for current use and also where I think all of this is going. So I want to start off with talking about LM Studio. So LM Studio is what I'm going to use for this. I'm also going to talk a little bit about how to do this in Olama."], [0, "But I want to focus on LM Studio because it's a little more visual. I think it's a little easier to get into. So LM Studio is an app. You could just go and download it. It's like lmstudio.ai or .com. I'm not sure exactly. It doesn't really matter. If you search for LM Studio, you're going to find it. It's going to be the first thing you see. So you download that."], [0, "I'm on a Mac. I think it runs on lots of different things, but it's mostly Mac-friendly, I think. All right, so you're going to download that. And what this does is it allows you to download different models. Now, if we come over here to the internet, we have basically Hugging Face is a place that I really, really enjoy going to get models. It's the place you want to be using for this type of thing."], [0, "because it allows you to download and mess with lots of different models. And if we actually go to just the models directory here, look at this. Over 1 million different AI models are now available. And these are all open source. You can just download and mess with these however you want."], [0, "So what I did was I went and looked on Reddit and said, hey, I want to write some fiction. I want to be able to write long stories and I want them to be high quality and blah, blah, blah. I basically said, hey, what's the best models?"], [0, "you know, open source models available today that you could use to write decent fiction. And they came back with a whole bunch of answers. And I tried a bunch of them and we'll see them in the list a little bit later on. But, so this is one I kind of settled on. We'll just go with this. There might be better ones. There might be, there's definitely worse ones. But this has got a few things that are pretty interesting. First of all, it's uncensored. So violence,"], [0, "like romantic stuff, you know, spicy stuff, essentially, any sorts of combat, things like that, that might be blocked by like a standard llama model or a standard open AI model or whatever."], [0, "Well, first of all, those open AI, you'd be charged for it. Second of all, there's lots of restrictions on them, which make them potentially, like, I don't think Harry Potter would pass the filters for a commercial model. So you got to think about, like, that's the reason we're using open source models. Second reason is because they're free other than..."], [0, "You have to have a machine that can run them. But other than that, it's like producing stuff for you for free. So that's the reason we're using open source models. And they keep getting better and better and better. So this particular model is based around Llama 3, Llama 3.1.8b Instruct. And the context size is quite large. So that's what we like about this."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["artificial intelligence", "podcasting", "security ai", "fiction writing", "open source models", "language models", "lm studio", "ollama", "hugging face", "model downloading", "llama models", "context length", "content moderation", "creative writing", "reddit forums", "mac software", "digital publishing", "technology ethics"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778637286.2846212, "duration_sec": 1.781855821609497, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "1,000%. You know what it is, Joe?"], [1, "And they're never good."], [0, "That\u2014 you know what it is too, Joe, is that deep down inside they."], [1, "Want to be that person."], [0, "100%."], [1, "And I tell you, or they want to be in the position that person's in, is a better way of putting it."], [0, "And I'll use this, I'll tell you this story. I realize that\u2014 not to say names, but you\u2014 it's so many people that could be guilty of And this is the thing that I hear that understands me, that disturbs me, is that, you know, a lot of these people that bitch the most, they at some point in their career, they were favored by Hollywood. At some point in their career, they had these opportunities. At some point in their career, guess what? They had the agencies. They had the agents. And something happened in their career where they they fell out of favor for whatever you want to call that, whatever you want to call that, for whatever. Maybe something they did, agency didn't like, like them too much. And now everything that they wrote on, everything they wanted to do, now it's all, that's fucked up. And the only way you get this opportunity because it has to be sexual favors and all that type of shit. And where did the fuck you draw the line?"], [1, "But it's not even sexual favors. It's like they criticize the work of the other person. That person ain't shit. That person sucks."], [0, "Can I, can I Yo, there's\u2014 here's the thing."], [1, "There was\u2014 come on, you get this all the time because you ride with Dave, and Dave's number one, right? So you always get\u2014 you always get this label, even though you're a great comic, you get this label being a coattail rider, right?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["hollywood", "entertainment industry", "career opportunities", "talent agencies", "agents", "industry favoritism", "jealousy", "resentment", "criticism", "sexual favors", "workplace politics", "comedy", "social status", "power dynamics", "reputation", "networking", "competition", "bias"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778637644.9958487, "duration_sec": 1.7320959568023682, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And would that be like all the products or like just the products that have been shown on pages that the person has visited? Because, you know, like it seems like not realistic for you to try and duplicate all of the contents of maybe your entire product database into memory on the client. Right."], [0, "Yeah, so that would just be every time you've gone and fetched the products, they're going to a product page or a product listing page. That's the point where you'd store that product information in the store. And then for the basket, for example, instead of storing the products again, all of that information, you just have the ID or an array of IDs that can come to you there. That means if you go to the basket page, for example,"], [0, "you would then go look at those IDs that currently sit in your basket and prefetch or receive all of those products data that you need to display, which means you're only fetching what you need and you're only using the state to build up your UI. You're not using it to clone your database because your database could be hundreds of gigabytes of data. Of course, you don't want to sort that on a client."], [0, "Just wanted to take a quick break to thank one of this week's sponsors and that's CodeChip. So CodeChip is a hosted continuous integration platform in the cloud that helps you increase your development productivity and ship to production more frequently. CodeChip lets you standardize your tooling and processes across your teams, speeds up your build times and integrates into your existing ecosystem of tools."], [0, "CodeChip is a great fit for your team, whether you're just trying to speed up the build times for large apps, or if you want to set up complex delivery pipelines for your microservices using tools like Docker, Kubernetes, and others. Forrester recently released their latest continuous integration tools report, which provides valuable guidance into the rapidly growing continuous integration and continuous delivery market."], [0, "And CodeChip actually scored as a top five continuous integration vendor in this report. If you're interested in reading this report and learning more about what makes for a great continuous integration and continuous delivery service, you can check out the show notes for this episode and I'll have a link there for you."], [0, "So if you want to spend less time managing your tools and speed up your software development, give CodeChip a try and sign up for free today at CodeChip.com. I've been a user of CodeChip for many years for all the open source projects that I run continuous integration on, as well as private projects where I UCI, and I couldn't be happier with the service. So thanks to CodeChip for sponsoring the podcast this week, and back to the show."], [0, "So I guess it sounds like we sort of implied this, I think, but like it sounds like your store is almost working sort of like a cache in some ways, like just like a local cache of information that you would have otherwise requested from the server, or at least that's like one type of state that you might be storing there. Yeah."], [0, "From a regular component to fetch information about an individual product, I'd be curious to know what that flow maybe looks like. Say, compared to what someone might do with a non-ViewX app, or maybe you have..."], [0, "you know, a component that uses a library like Axios or something to go and directly fetch that information from the API. How does that look differently when you want to get that information from Vuex? Is Vuex responsible for making the API request? Is it sort of trying to hide those implementation details from you? Just be curious to know how that kind of looks and works."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["vuex", "state management", "client caching", "product catalog", "shopping cart", "product ids", "api requests", "data fetching", "memory usage", "user interface", "single page apps", "client server", "continuous integration", "delivery pipelines", "cloud platforms", "docker", "kubernetes", "development productivity"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778638745.682851, "duration_sec": 2.317509889602661, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "Dental?"], [1, "Pharmacy?"], [2, "Glasses?"], [3, "You know what I did all day? I talked nonstop all day. And I still have a little energy left."], [1, "Alberto loves to talk. When I told him I wanted to talk about Molar City, he was like, \"How much time do you have?\" He told me how one time he was on the phone with a girlfriend for a whole 24 hours, standing at a payphone for an entire day. An entire day just talking. From this anecdote alone, I got the impression that Alberto was probably good at his job, or at least that he had the stamina to endure it. Well, maybe let's not aim for 24 hours, but I'd love to chat a little bit. Alberto grew up nearby, and over the course of his life, he's seen how Los Algodones transformed into Molar City."], [3, "Being the border, we have this\u2014 we have America. America is very influential. America really affects everything that happens to the people right here."], [1, "Los Algodones has built itself up to serve the dental needs of Americans and Canadians. And the town has become wildly successful in this goal. But Algodones went through many reinventions before it became molar city. And I wanted to understand how it all happened and why. Los Algodones means cotton in plural. And that's because commercial cotton farming is what this land was once known for, back when it was still vibrant and green. Then a series of policy changes depleted the soil and dammed the flow of the Colorado River into Mexico. By the '60s, riverbeds were drying up. And crops were struggling. But many Mexican border towns have long had another thriving business: booze. And after the farming industry crumbled, that's what Los Algodones relied on. At one point, the town had 48 bars. American soldiers living on military bases in Yuma, Arizona, were regulars. Alberto spent his childhood in Yuma, too."], [3, "Yeah, we'd go to Algodones to get drunk. But yeah, I just\u2014 that's what Agua Dulce has been to me from the beginning. It was just a little town."], [2, "Agua Dulce was a very small town with lots of cantinas, and used to come people from Yuma to dance in the cantinas or drink."], [1, "This is Dr. Jesus Medina. He moved to town fresh out of med school in 1973, and he's been working here as a doctor ever since, although he seems to be perpetually on the brink of retirement."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["dentistry", "dental tourism", "border town", "los algodones", "molar city", "americans", "canadians", "cotton farming", "colorado river", "policy changes", "bars", "alcoholism", "healthcare", "tourism industry", "cross border trade", "environmental change", "military bases", "retirement"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778639706.5507684, "duration_sec": 2.3447954654693604, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So both are real and we don't block you because of the technology choice from having a suitable pipeline which automates your testing, automates your telemetry, automates your feedback. So you keep on saying the word telemetry. I don't know if that's well defined for our listeners. Will you explain that a little bit?"], [0, "Sure, so by telemetry I mean the instrumentation that collects data from production and gives you things like insights about production performance, about server logs, about crashes, about command patterns, everything about visibility into what's running in production."], [0, "The analogy of telemetry initially came up from the notion that you had a view on harder machinery,"], [0, "In software, the idea is that everything you deploy, be it server side or device side, you want to have visibility into, both for understanding usage and for troubleshooting, because your goal is to minimize your time to detect, your time to mitigate,"], [0, "and your time to remediate. And to do that, you need to see what's going on. So you're also talking about data that's coming from Android devices, iPhone devices, browsers? Yes. Yeah, so we have, for Android, iOS, and so forth, we've had Hockey app, and that's now part of the data you get in the Visual Studio Mobile. So that will give you"], [0, "you know, direct crash dumps, it'll give you logs, it'll give you usage patterns. For server side or service side, we have application insights and application insights analytics. We use this, for example, ourselves across Azure services. We're collecting right now about 1.6 petabytes of data a day using telemetry. Oh, is that all?"], [0, "Yeah, so the, yeah, so, I mean, it grows every month by a lot. So, you know, we don't keep it forever, but if someone needs to troubleshoot an instance"], [0, "of a customer account with anything, that data's all available to them. So, you know, in 10 years ago or more, you would have had this argument about can the developer get access to the production box. Now it's not a question because instead of needing to get access to the production box, all the production data is brought to the developer and you can keep the production environment secure."], [0, "I had that fight less than 10 years ago. Well, there you go. Yeah. And I think depending on where you're working and who you're working with, that's still a concern. But it's nice to see it move the other way because it makes it much, much easier for all of the things that have to work together to work together. Yeah. And I mean, one of the things that is also a very big concern right now is..."], [0, "everywhere you go is security. And the, you know, ever since the target CEO got fired five years ago and then Sony got breached and then the US government personnel office got breached and everyone with a security clearance discovered their social security number and their kids' bios were in China, people have been much more conscious about how does"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["telemetry", "instrumentation", "production monitoring", "automated testing", "deployment pipeline", "feedback loops", "crash reporting", "server logs", "performance insights", "usage analytics", "troubleshooting", "security", "microsoft azure", "visual studio mobile", "hockeyapp", "data privacy", "incident response", "access control"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778639853.8500674, "duration_sec": 2.5189054012298584, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Hey everyone and welcome to Developer Tea. My name is Jonathan Cottrell and in today's short episode, I'm going to be giving you a simple tip to make people like your estimates a little bit more. I'm sure that you have experienced this just as much as I have if you have been working in software development for a long enough time, but it tends to be the case that I overestimate my abilities and"], [0, "and I end up needing to estimate higher. And that always frustrates people when I estimate that I'm going to be able to get something done in five days, and then it takes me seven. I've mentioned on the show before that estimation is notoriously difficult. In fact, we are incredibly bad at estimating things, particularly on a timescale."], [0, "It turns out that we are okay at estimating things on a relative scale, but we're pretty bad on estimating them in an absolute scale. So there's a lot of intricacies involved with estimation. And in fact, I recommend trying to move away from estimation when possible. But even still, you're going to need to give some kind of number to explain what it takes for somebody to get you to do some amount of work."], [0, "That is what an estimation is. That is what a quote is. Somebody else providing you money for the work that you do. Now, we aren't just bad at estimation in that we are all over the board. The way in which we are bad at estimation is that we typically undershoot"], [0, "our projects. We typically think that we can get way more done in a given amount of time. I did an episode about estimating sandwiches. Go and check it out if you want to learn a little bit more about why that is the case, about why we tend to underestimate the amount of effort required to get something done."], [0, "But the reality is that we tend to underestimate the amount of energy it takes to get something done. And as it turns out, this happens with just about everything that takes longer than half a day. We're pretty bad at estimating anything that takes longer than half a day. We can actually estimate half a day tasks pretty well. But if we're trying to estimate a larger project, something that's going to take a week or something like that,"], [0, "we typically underestimate the amount of energy necessary to accomplish whatever that project is. So how do we avoid upsetting people? How do we avoid a situation where a client or a boss or a customer thinks that we're trying to cheat them out of money or thinks that we're being lazy? How can we avoid these situations? Well, the first one, of course, is to act with integrity. This is a very important part of"], [0, "of today's episode. Act with integrity, so don't be lazy. That is important for other people to believe that you aren't lazy. You need to not be lazy, of course. The second part has to do with your estimation process. And very simply, you need to not underestimate your"], [0, "in order to avoid a situation where you have to tell that client or that boss that you've underestimated the amount of time and that it's going to take longer than expected. I'm going to take a quick sponsor break and then I'm going to come back and give you some information about a cognitive bias that can actually work in your favor in this arena, especially when you feel that you have done everything right and things are still going wrong. Right after this sponsor break, we're going to talk about how to fix this problem."], [0, "Thanks so much to today's sponsor, Harvest. One thing that all great programmers and really anybody who's successful has in common is that they know where their time is going and they spend it intentionally. Now ask yourself, do you know where your time is going every day?"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["software development", "project estimation", "time management", "effort estimation", "underestimation", "overestimation", "relative estimation", "absolute scale", "project planning", "client expectations", "integrity", "productivity", "cognitive bias", "behavioral psychology", "consulting", "time tracking", "billing", "decision making"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
