king with government buyers, we give you access to the most relevant bid opportunities so you can focus on winning more government business. Register Now"], [2, "ARTICLE 87 - SMART GROWTH OVERLAY DISTRICTS - Municode Library\nThis article sets forth the procedures and minimum requirements for the creation of Smart Growth Overlay Districts (SGODs) within the City of Boston in accordance with Chapter 40R of the Massachusetts General Laws and Section 3-1A of this Code. The purposes of an SGOD are to (a) promote the public health, safety, and welfare by encouraging diversity of housing opportunities; (b) increase the ..."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": null, "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["boston", "massachusetts law", "zoning regulations", "land use", "urban planning", "housing policy", "affordable housing", "housing supply", "housing development", "multifamily housing", "smart growth", "overlay districts", "municipal code", "local government", "government accountability", "congress oversight", "federal spending", "procurement", "request for proposal", "government contracts", "competitive bidding"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778640232.5652215, "duration_sec": 1.9899964332580566, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And that's going to surprise a lot of people because Next.js sells server-side rendering really hard. You know, we do this for you and it gives you some benefits and there are some benefits there. But some of the problems with server-side rendering is your application is not actually functional and useful in the browser until you've downloaded that expensive piece of JavaScript and you've hydrated your application."], [0, "So yes, you will get that first paint really quickly and you'll see some content on the screen, but nothing's going to work. None of your buttons are going to work. Your site's not interactive until you've downloaded the JavaScript bundle and hydrated that application. So there's a moment of frustration for the user that happens there when you're staring at something, but it's not working. And so maybe you solve that with loading screens and whatnot."], [0, "One of the bigger problems with server-side rendering that I encountered at a fairly large company is that it doesn't scale well, or we couldn't find a way to get it to scale nicely for high traffic sites. You have to realize that to render React on the server, you need to use Node.js. That's the only way to run JavaScript outside of a browser is using Node.js."], [0, "So depending on whatever your server is, and we didn't have node servers in our case, but even if you did have a node server, I think you would still have this problem. So first of all, we didn't have a node server. Our web servers were typically running .NET sort of stuff. So a request would come in to say, hey, I want this web page."], [0, ".NET would go, okay, I need to fire up a Node instance. I need to say someone's looking for this route. Node would have to fire up our React application, render it, send it back to .NET, and then .NET would send the server-side rendered version of the page plus the JavaScript application back down to the client. So right away, you can go, hey, there's this step you have that's kind of expensive getting in the way, and it's .NET firing up Node automatically."], [0, "just to render a page and then sending that back. So let's just assume for this example that we don't have a dotnet server, we're talking straight to node. So node is our web server. And a request comes in and says, Hey, I want this page. No, it says, Okay, fine. I need to boot up this react application, do a render and send it back to you. The problem is that node and all JavaScript is single threaded, which means it can only do one thing at a time can't do anything else. Now on a high traffic website,"], [0, "And I said, renders are expensive, remember. So you got a request coming in. Node says, okay, I need to stop everything else and I need to handle the rendering of this application. On a high traffic website, if another request comes in while Node is doing that render, then it has to wait. So it gets queued up."], [0, "And on a really high traffic website, like the one I was working on, a couple hundred thousand requests would show up during the rendering of this React application. So now these couple hundred thousand requests are waiting because there's only one node instance that can only do one thing at a time. This is a big scaling problem. So to get around that, you can scale up node. You can run one node per core of CPU that you have on your web server. So you can throw money at the problem and just increase the number of web servers you have, increase the number of cores on the web servers you have."], [0, "But you end up getting into round robin problems. So to do that properly, you have to have some sort of scheduler built into your web server system saying, OK, I need to find a node instance that's free. And I'm going to send this request to that node instance. And then the next request I'm going to send to the next free node instance that I have. And when I finally run out of node instances, then I have to use some sort of queue. This makes the whole problem way more complicated. And all you're doing is you're"], [0, "What are you saving? Google can handle your client-side rendered application perfectly fine. And Googlebot is the one bot you really need to care about in your website. So that problem's kind of been solved. Even if you are sending down a server-side rendered page to the user, they're still going to experience this moment of frustration because they still have to download your JavaScript application in order for this static page to become interactive and useful."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["server side rendering", "nextjs", "react", "nodejs", "hydration", "javascript bundle", "client side rendering", "web performance", "interactivity", "scaling", "high traffic", "load balancing", "dotnet", "single threaded", "request queue", "search engine optimization", "infrastructure", "user experience"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778640574.110332, "duration_sec": 1.6364595890045166, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "And I start to work and I start to see American people, you know, hard time because I don't speak too much English, but I don't kill nobody, you know."], [1, "Maga\u00f1a and Medina were treating patients who would talk about their struggles with healthcare in America. For most Americans, dental care has always been out of reach. Early dentistry was considered more of a craftsman's trade. Dental work was performed by barbers and blacksmiths, people who were considered quacks by the larger medical field. And ever since then, dentistry and general medicine have fought hard to keep themselves separate. Cut to today, and dental and healthcare are still separated. They have separate insurances, separate teaching schools, separate medical records, even. It's as if matters of the mouth are separate from the body. But of course, they're not. Every year, hundreds of thousands of Americans end up in emergency rooms for something like a toothache, toothache, something that could have been treated in a dental office. But most ERs don't have on-call dentists, so often these patients are sent home with painkillers and are told to go visit their dentist. But a lot of these people don't have dentists, or if they do, the cost for a visit is impossibly high. And yet people are held personally accountable for the state of their teeth in ways that they're not held accountable for many other health conditions. Poor dental care can be both life-threatening and humiliating, as if unhealthy teeth are a failure of individual responsibility rather than the symptom of a broken system. In Algodones, Dr. Maga\u00f1a saw the state of dental care in America as an opportunity to turn this small Mexican border town into a place full of dental services catered to desperate Americans and Canadians. Looking for deep discounts. But the town was still mostly strip clubs and bars, and he felt like that was holding them back. So in 1980, Maga\u00f1a became mayor, although the technically accurate term is municipal delegate. As municipal delegate, Maga\u00f1a shuttered most of the cantinas. It was his first major step in transforming Algodones into Molar City. Year after year, the same buildings that housed those cantinas gave way to more clinics and pharmacies."], [2, "Cantinas, I don't have there no more. Only one or two in Algodones, you know. And, but they disappear almost, you know. If we drink, we drink at home. All cantinas are dental offices now."], [1, "Maga\u00f1a helped transform the town in other ways, too. He opened a middle school, a high school, and a dental school. And Dr. Medina, like his brother-in-law, also got involved in town leadership. He coached the local sports teams, and he became the town's first official delegate of tourism."], [2, "And when I come, we make the revolution right here, you know?"], [1, "More and more healthcare professionals were migrating to Algodones, and among Americans, knowledge of the town's services was spreading, although mostly by word of mouth. Still, Dr. Medina was looking for a way to take Algodones to the next level. And so he decided to attract people to their reformed party town by throwing a giant party, a party specifically for snowbirds, a term for retired Americans and Canadians who migrate south every year following the sun. \"Come on over,\" Medina advertised. \"We'll have free margaritas, free Viagra, discounted root canals.\" When kind of did you start this?"], [2, "Wait a minute, because I forgot. Right here is when they gave me the position, you know, in the newspaper of Mexicali."], [1, "Dr. Medina is sitting in his office, flipping through an old photo album that he's holding up to our Zoom screen. It's full of newspaper clippings and pictures of all the parties he threw over the years. His first one was in 1987. 7,000 tourists showed up. In the photos, they're all crammed into the town's four-square blocks. Many are holding red Solo cups."], [2, "This was my first party. Look at American people, how they doing just in the street, you know? I don't have places to do it."], [1, "Medina flips through the book fondly, as if he's cooing at baby photos."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["dental care", "healthcare access", "emergency rooms", "insurance", "medical records", "dentistry history", "border town", "mexico", "tourism", "dental clinics", "discounts", "community leadership", "public health", "healthcare inequality", "migration", "retirement", "local economy", "marketing"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
