{"timestamp": 1778641223.685164, "duration_sec": 2.0612683296203613, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "That's what I call it. Life dollars to get to school, gas vouchers, you know, a tire blows, you know, the rent is due, you know, and they have to work. The objective is to get them to the other side, get them certified, finish your training, get to work. Absolutely. Get more money, maybe get a higher paying job, you know, upskill, reskill, whatever that is. We need to get them there."], [0, "Last one of the last conversations went toward last year was a fundraiser for that foundation. Yes. To pay for those life emergencies. Yes. To make sure that fill in the gaps. You keep studying. Take your tests. Go get certified. That's how much money did you raise? So we raised through the gala and also through now noble and actually with our new friends, our windmill friends. OK. They've also contributed. We've raised a little over forty thousand dollars."], [0, "So so that's and of course, these are expenses that are not over a thousand. These are like two hundred, three hundred dollars. So we've been able to help with families and some of these students. Yeah, they can't afford that. Exactly. And it's just like our food pantry and our, you know, closet, you know, whenever they need anything there, it's there for them."], [0, "And a quick mention for the Jaguar Alumni Initiative. Yes. I mean, this is something. It's called Share the Love. It's Valentine's Day, February 14th. Instead of sending me flowers, do a donation, right? Go to southtexascollege.edu and pledge. I mean, there's a lot of folks that walk through the doors of South Texas College. We're trying to outreach our alumni. We sent out over 15,000 postcards and texts and emails hoping that we get our alumni back. Yeah."], [0, "We're really hoping for a great success there. Freddie still wants flowers and chocolate. Yeah, he's the romantic. Good to see you, Dr. Rodney. Great seeing you. Come back and visit soon on so many wonderful things. Dr. Rodney Rodriguez, Vice President from South Texas College. Thank you."], [0, "We'll be right back."], [0, "It's called the Radiopotami app. Find it in your Google Play or Apple App Store. From your friends at News Talk 710 KURV."], [0, "You're listening to the best of the Valley's morning news. Here's Sergio. We have a WhatsApp number. If you'd like to check in with us. No, don't call it. I cannot say that. Don't call. I was going to answer. But you can WhatsApp a message via video, audio, or text it to us. The 710 KURV WhatsApp. 956-862-2482. That's 862-2482."], [0, "It's election season again, and early voting is underway for the Texas primary. March 3rd is the Texas primary, but we've got a couple of weeks of early vote. You can beat the crowd. And as is customary, as we have a big election..."], [0, "Now let's go to our elections administrators for Hidalgo County and for Cameron County. Hilda Salinas for Hidalgo County and Remy Garza for Cameron County. Since we cleared day one already, let's look at some of the day one numbers. So Remy, let's start with you. Cameron County, what you got for early vote numbers day one. What does that look like?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["student support", "emergency assistance", "workforce training", "certification exams", "upskilling", "reskilling", "fundraising gala", "charitable foundation", "alumni outreach", "college donations", "food pantry", "resource closet", "texas primary", "early voting", "election administration", "nonprofit management", "higher education", "civic participation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778641466.3190088, "duration_sec": 2.423806667327881, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And in order to not invest in a healthy life, in other words, to kind of maintain the status quo, assuming that you are not doing any of those things may have a very low cost, at least right now. And this illustrates a good example of considering the cost curves."], [0, "What is the cost over time? Well, if you invest in healthy habits now, most of those habits will have lower overhead as you begin to kind of create systems that support those habits. It becomes a part of your life. You begin to kind of integrate it with your other activities. And so the cost kind of goes down a little bit over time. That investment overall is going to be a small ongoing cost probably throughout the rest of your life."], [0, "The alternative might mean a curve that stays relatively flat and then starts to go up. As you age those investments that you could have made in your health if they weren't made, your health may start declining and costing you money, comfort, time, functionality, whatever it is. Over time, if you don't invest in your health, becoming unhealthy will"], [0, "The cost curve goes dramatically up to the right. And so it's important to look at these and decide which one of these seems like the best one for me."], [0, "Keep in mind that there are two kind of ways of looking at cost curves. The first is the relative consideration. So this is kind of looking at the theoretical side of the cost curve. That is, if you had an infinite amount of money, which of the curves is best for you? On the other hand, you could look at the absolute curve. The absolute curve actually assigns numbers."], [0, "This is most relevant, of course, when you're talking about actual money. For example, you might look at cost curves between buying or renting a home. And initially, the curve itself, if you were to kind of normalize it, make them relative to each other, the curve of buying seems attractive, especially if you could buy all up front. You're going to spend less money, generally speaking, purchasing a home than you would renting for the rest of your life."], [0, "However, if you look at the absolute cost of that, most people can't just buy a home outright. And so even though that one-time expense would yield a better cost curve or a more kind of amenable cost curve over the course of your life, it also has some absolute limits."], [0, "And so we need to keep this in mind when we're looking at different cost options, different cost curves. What is it that makes sense for us? Not just in theory, but with our actual situation, with our actual resources, what makes the most sense? You can also apply the same concepts to buy versus build decisions."], [0, "A buy decision might smooth out your costs over time. You purchase something off the shelf, it reduces the cost, but you keep paying that same cost indefinitely. Whereas if you build something, you pay a pretty high upfront cost, but then you start paying less over time."], [0, "Remember, the cost curve of building something also includes the variability of what happens when it breaks or what is the cost of maintenance, while the cost curve of buying something is only volatile related to whether or not the provider of that particular thing is going to sustain their business and whether they will change their prices once you've already bought in."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cost curves", "healthy habits", "health investment", "aging", "long term costs", "status quo", "relative costs", "absolute costs", "buying vs renting", "home ownership", "buy vs build", "maintenance costs", "personal finance", "decision making", "risk management", "budgeting", "real estate", "business sustainability"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778641793.1145606, "duration_sec": 2.0876545906066895, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "To just work locally on someone's computer, even though you're using like web technologies, like what are the sorts of things that you have to take into account and consider that someone who's used to just building stuff on the web normally doesn't have to think about so much?"], [0, "There's a lot. I'm still kind of mulling around these ideas, so I might not be extremely eloquent yet. But one of the main things is just that if you want to build something local, I mean, for the web, you kind of just guess that your data might be there, it might not. And we kind of hack around the fact that..."], [0, "your network connection might be slow or you might not have one by having these caches that's like if we've already loaded this item we're going to stick into the cache and hey and then you you go offline and everything is still if the website is still actually there in your browser you could at least like click on the item again and it's still there but if you're building it local I mean honestly more often than not I have there are things that I don't have to deal with that the web has to deal with because I I can just assume that all of the items are already there"], [0, "So it's an interesting question because I'm trying to think like, I think what you need to figure out is basically how, just how to make the app run when it's offline. Because honestly, in my opinion, it's not actually a truly local offline app if I can't restart my computer, have everything not running, boot it up from a cold start, and everything is just there. I mean, so it's, I mean,"], [0, "I mean, there's a lot you have to think about, right? I mean, it's pretty much a re-architecture of making everything local so that it just works that way."], [0, "Yeah, there's definitely a lot of web apps that I use that have quote-unquote offline support, but the way that you achieve that is, okay, before you get on the plane, make sure that you have it open in your browser and that it's there waiting so that when network access is lost, everything is still booted up and ready to go. But something that works like a native macOS application or something where, yeah, I can open the Notes app while I'm on an airplane and..."], [0, "I don't have to make any considerations about it at all. That's a whole different sort of ballgame. So maybe a good place to start would be talking about some of the technology choices that you made when putting this thing together and then digging deeper into..."], [0, "How certain things even work, like like what you're doing to store the data locally, because I know there's all sorts of different web technologies for that. And in Electron, you have like direct access to the file system. So you have all sorts of other ways that you can handle this sort of thing."], [0, "So I guess to start, how is the app built? Are you using React or Vue or something else? What are all the pieces in play there? Sure. So it uses Electron for the native to wrap a web technology app into a"], [0, "a native desktop app. So all of the files are local, obviously. And so the app itself can at least always boot. And there's actually also a mobile app as well. So I'll always kind of talk about that just a little bit. And that's a native iOS app. I don't have Android yet, but that's written in React Native. And so that it won't be too hard to port it to Android once I find time. And so React is kind of the backbone of all of this as well, because mobile app is written in React Native and the desktop app is written in React Native."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["offline apps", "local storage", "web applications", "desktop apps", "electron", "file system access", "caching", "network connectivity", "cold start", "native apps", "react", "react native", "progressive web apps", "ios development", "android porting", "software architecture", "cross platform development", "offline first design"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
