{"timestamp": 1778646929.4281592, "duration_sec": 2.1525073051452637, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Greetings and welcome to Unsupervised Learning. I'm Daniel Miesler, and this show provides content curation as a service with a focus on the intersection of security, technology, and humans. I spend between five and 20 hours a week consuming books, articles, and podcasts. And each episode is either a curated summary of what I've discovered in the past week or a standalone essay that hopefully gives you something to think about."], [0, "All right, welcome to episode 182. Starting off with security news, the US is supposedly ramping up attacks against the Russian power grid through the use of new cyber attack powers granted by Trump. I'm happy to hear of this, but it's an example of where we as outsiders can only know a tiny fragment of the story. But I think any signs that this administration sees Russia as a foe and is treating it as such is positive in my view."], [0, "Adobe is entering the deepfakes arena by showing off research tools designed to detect manipulated photos. Target stores have been hit by major outages. So for a couple days in a row, registers were not working. Although they seem to be working here in California, at least in the Bay Area. But evidently it's been pretty widespread. And this is the first time that I can remember that they've been in the news since the big breaches."], [0, "Many places are using very granular Bluetooth beacon tracking to watch you move throughout their businesses, including airports, malls, subways, buses, gyms, hotels, festivals, museums, etc. The US is going after ethnic Chinese researchers in the medical field and specifically at cancer centers. I'm all for becoming more aggressive towards the Chinese government pilfering the world's intellectual property."], [0, "But I think cancer research is one thing that I think it's okay to spread widely."], [0, "It's not like they're stealing the only copy of the research. They're just sharing it, albeit illegally. Maybe I'm missing something though. But if this is just a matter of profit, so basically they can develop a drug before we can. I don't know. I'm kind of calling Matt on this because we can still build that product and sell it as far as I could tell. So even if they can as well, I think that's fine. I mean, if we were..."], [0, "Built a product and sold it and it was really expensive and they built a cheaper version and it got out there. Like I'm just finding a hard time seeing a negative, a real negative come from too much cancer research or cancer research being proliferated."], [0, "Firewalling outbound DNS could save companies billions. I absolutely agree. I've been on about this for years. Samsung is telling people to scan their TVs for viruses every once in a while, manually. That is not the way any of this is supposed to work. We're getting very close to the point where Trump or someone like him will claim that a very clear video of him saying something horrible or stupid is a deep fake."], [0, "That moment will be a clear milestone in the decay of our orbit around insanity. Momentum Cyber's May snapshot. This is always a great, great thing to take a look at."], [0, "The hack back debate is alive and well. I think I'm mostly against, but one, I don't know enough about it. And two, I think there probably are situations where it's okay. I just, you have to have a lot of trust in the people thinking about this. And right now I don't have that much trust in that."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cybersecurity", "technology", "content curation", "cyber attacks", "power grid", "russia", "deepfakes", "image manipulation", "retail outages", "bluetooth tracking", "intellectual property", "dns firewalling", "us china relations", "medical research", "privacy", "digital forensics", "regulation", "decision making"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
