{"timestamp": 1778647180.5188046, "duration_sec": 2.121744394302368, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And so Lieber is a is a tragic villain in this case, because he did it for the research and the money. He got paid a lot of money, great money. But, you know, he ultimately made America weaker. I think we're going to know the extent of the kind of damage he did to our national security and our economy for decades to come."], [0, "The Wikipedia tells us, following a week-long trial on December 21st, 2021, Mr. Lieber was found guilty on all charges, two counts of making false statements to the U.S. government, two counts of filing a false income tax return. This has to do with the money that..."], [0, "He was provided by the People's Republic of China and two counts of failing to report foreign bank accounts. He was sentenced in the spring of 2023. It's the fall of a very public man for the Thaos and Talens program. And now we move to another example. It's not all about COVID-19."], [0, "This has to do with what is called the CRISPR. That is a genius invention of these last years, clustered regularly interspersed short palindromic repeats, CRISPR, C-R-I-S-P-R, and a Chinese scientist named He. What did he do and what do we know about him? Yeah."], [0, "Right. So CRISPR-Cas9 is the official designation, and basically that is the tool that geneticists use to splice DNA, to go in. We use this to map the human genome, and with that knowledge of the map, we now can..."], [0, "splice genes and input things into genes, take things out that are undesirable, theoretically, and into genetic makeup of people, animals, diseases. And He Jiankui was a Chinese geneticist who was using CRISPR. By the way, CRISPR is an American invention, and basically we gave access to it to the Chinese, like we give China everything. And so He Jiankui was doing experiments trying to remove HIV in utero, in the womb, and"], [0, "of a woman who carried HIV and had passed it on to her babies in the womb. And so what Hu Jankui did was he tried to use CRISPR to genetically remove the HIV genes or the susceptibility to HIV in the unborn twins. And it worked."], [0, "And so on the one level, you say good for him. He might have helped to cure a lot of, you know, you know, diseases and young children with this methodology. But what they found when the children were born were that there were other augmentations, whether it was intentional or unintentional. Either way, it's scary. There were other augmentations to the children. The children are healthy as far as we know. But who junk? We was punished by the Chinese government."], [0, "For having done this supposedly without permission, although I suspect it was he was punished because the West found out and it became a viral story, if you'll pardon the expression here in the West. And so the Chinese were trying to tamp down on us finding out just what they were doing with CRISPR, because if that's what they were doing with CRISPR, a genetic manipulation."], [0, "uh at the in utero that's just the tip of the iceberg as i show in my book they were probably doing a lot more with crisper and they were trying to keep western uh you know eyes off of the extent to what they were doing with crisper cas9 i believe in your book and your report you say that in some fashion his manipulation his frankenstein will augmented their brains their thinking yes"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["national security", "espionage", "china", "academic research", "trial", "false statements", "tax fraud", "foreign bank accounts", "crispr cas9", "gene editing", "dna splicing", "human embryo", "bioethics", "genetic enhancement", "hiv prevention", "scientific innovation", "government regulation", "international relations"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778647547.414093, "duration_sec": 1.8686902523040771, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I can think of at least one friend who brings up how he one time shipped a bug that crashed on launch on every single user's device and it somehow made it through the app store, which boggles my mind. But that's a problem, right? If the app crashes on launch and before the crash reporting stuff even gets a chance to actually report the crash, you're kind of in bad shape, right? I mean, hopefully you can reproduce it on your device, but..."], [0, "So that's kind of cool. So this is just sort of like another level of verification, another smoke test so that you don't have that problem at least go out. Yes, exactly. And the value here is to have it part of your process so that you don't even think about it, right? Most of the time..."], [0, "Hopefully it will be useless because stuff just works, but you want it to be useful for that one time that it doesn't work on that brand of devices that you didn't have the opportunity to test on. So if I want to set up continuous integration, CI, on my app, is this available for Objective-C, Swift, Xamarin? Yes. So all three platforms. How do I set it up for, say, a native iOS app?"], [0, "So for a native iOS app, you just go to Bubble Center, you create an app, you say it's iOS, and then you say whether it's Objective-C or Swift, and you're ready to go. So it's really not just summary. OK, does it work? So say I want to run a bunch of unit tests. Does it work out of the box based on the test target?"], [0, "Yeah, so in your projects, if you have unit tests defined and you check that option, they will be run automatically. Okay. What if there's some sort of configuration or customization of the build environment or the testing environment? What kind of customization is possible?"], [0, "So that's a great question. And what we've been trying to do is to really simplify the process. So having something that you can set up very quickly, which accounts for most options. Now, definitely, as you say, you might want to customize like some credentials or things like that, definitely in your build process. So that's something that we don't have just yet, but we're working on and should be released pretty soon. So definitely the requirement to be able to"], [0, "at build time, define some specific target environments, for example, environment variables and things like that is super high demand, so that's definitely something we've been working on and should be out soon. Okay, well, that's a good, honest answer. So CI, we talk about CI, we're talking about constantly building and running tests anytime somebody pushes code, a new build runs and tests run, and you catch bugs quickly, and you catch things that break the build quickly."], [0, "But there's also this part of it that sometimes gets slumped together, that's CD, continuous deployment. Is that something you... There's CI, but there's also CD, continuous deployment. What does your support for that look like? So the whole point of defining a kind of a flow with Mobile Center is that from the build definition, you select a branch, you set up your build test,"], [0, "And you set up which distribution group will receive that build. So if you want that, this will automatically happen. So you just set up who is going to be in that distribution group. And then automatically, we will just send the build to these testers. So they can be internal people, external. You just have to invite the people into your distribution group."], [0, "They don't even have to have access to your environment or whatever. Just invite them with an email address, and they can be in that distribution group. Are you using, I don't know if you're still calling it Hockey App. Well, yeah. Actually, Mobile Center is like Hockey App V2. So that's really the future of Hockey App is Mobile Center. So we are getting all the things that are today in Hockey App into Mobile Center, and we want to go much further. Okay, cool. Make it much better even."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["mobile apps", "app crashes", "crash reporting", "smoke testing", "continuous integration", "build automation", "unit testing", "ios development", "swift", "objective c", "xamarin", "continuous deployment", "release management", "beta distribution", "app store review", "environment variables", "software quality", "devops pipelines"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778648890.050472, "duration_sec": 2.6712534427642822, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And you're listening to NPR News. The children of the late civil rights activist the Reverend Jesse Jackson spoke publicly one day after their father's death. NPR's Sheryl Corley reports 5 of his 6 children were present and reflected on Jackson's life and legacy: Jackson's eldest, his daughter Santita, said some in the family thought her father would not live past 39\u2014that was the age of his mentor, Dr."], [5, "Martin Luther King Jr., when he was assassinated. But Jackson continued that her father had an assignment from King to continue his work of fighting for civil rights. There is no one who's been more faithful to the mission of Reverend Dr. Martin Luther King than Reverend Jesse Louis Jackson Sr. She said now the family will continue Jackson's work of combating racism, fighting poverty and registering voters, and they call on others to do the same. Sheryl Corley, NPR News, Chicago."], [0, "President Trump's top economic adviser suggested Wednesday that Federal Reserve economists should be punished for research that shows that American companies and consumers are paying nearly all of the tariffs the White House imposed last year. Kevin Hassett's comments referred to a New York Fed research paper. In a CNBC interview, Hassett said the paper is an embarrassment. Its findings, however, have been echoed elsewhere. Some of the regional financial markets in Asia remain closed for the Lunar New Year holiday, but stocks in Japan are advancing, tracking a Wall Street rally. Led by the chipmaker NVIDIA. NVIDIA 