{"timestamp": 1778649501.835885, "duration_sec": 2.4171335697174072, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "The caveat is these terms tend to be what people look for when they're searching for someone like you, when they're searching for a developer. In particular, if you're working in an agency, you're likely going to have people searching responsive web design to find a firm or find an agency that will do web design for them."], [0, "And every industry has these key terms. These are terms that kind of signal various belief systems about how the work should be executed. They kind of point towards your values as a company."], [0, "If you value adaptive, responsive web design, then the people who are looking for you, the people who are searching these terms, they know that you're on the leading edge of the industry. That's kind of the heuristic that they're using. They're trying to use these words as a way to find someone who is credible."], [0, "Now, whether that is good or bad, we aren't really going to debate on this episode, but it certainly is still the case. So don't go and try to rip all that stuff out of your website. And certainly don't look down on your marketing manager or on the person who's responsible for your content management"], [0, "Don't look down on them for trying to utilize these words in meaningful ways. They are not meaningless words, and they are not just full of fluff. What this anti-resolution is about is trying to use those words in your everyday conversation when you're building a product, when you're actually working, when you're trying to communicate about, for example, a problem that you're having."], [0, "try your best to frame that problem in the most simple language possible. Go to the lowest common denominator that you can, and hopefully this will help you kind of translate between you and a coworker, you and a client, you and a manager, your boss. That translation process is extremely important, and the simpler language you use, the better."], [0, "The second anti-resolution, and this is the hardest one of the three probably, but the second anti-resolution is quit being busy. Quit being busy. There's quite a few podcast episodes on this subject that are coming out right now, but the idea here is that busy is very often a result of your own decisions. I challenge you to write out your entire schedule for a given day."], [0, "And I want you to look at this schedule and I want you to identify the most important, we'll use the Pareto principle here, it's very commonly used in these kinds of discussions, the most important 20% of events or activities that you participate in for that day."], [0, "For example, you might actually identify that cooking breakfast is extremely important to you. So you want to protect the time to cook your breakfast. But you may also identify that later on in the day, you spent some time watching TV or maybe something more productive. You spent some time cleaning your house. You may identify that that wasn't really that important to you, that you doing the cleaning work yourself is not really important to you."], [0, "that if you could offload that, if you could hire someone to do it for you, then you would. Now, I'm not by any means saying that you need to outsource all of the manual labor in your life. Very often, these manual labor tasks that we have to do as developers, they can be healthy for us because most of the work that we're doing is not manual labor. We're not really moving around very much in our day job. So it's a good break. It's a good physical break, a good mental break,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["responsive design", "web development", "digital agency", "marketing keywords", "search terms", "industry terminology", "company values", "credibility", "client communication", "simple language", "productivity", "time management", "content management", "pareto principle", "outsourcing", "manual labor", "decision making", "work life balance"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
