{"timestamp": 1778650118.4586792, "duration_sec": 1.9454150199890137, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Drinkag1.Com/Rogan."], [1, "Salt is an essential mineral. You need salt to survive. Salt is not the problem. They associate salt with high blood pressure, salt with this, salt with\u2014 it's not true."], [0, "It's bullshit. What type of salt? Is it a different salt?"], [1, "Is it like\u2014 no, it's not. Salt's not bad for you. Well, first of all, there's iodized salt, which is actually good for you because it contains iodine. They add iodine to it, which is good for you. But salt is not a bad thing. I mean, you shouldn't have too much salt. Look, if you eat enough salt, you could die."], [0, "Joe, you can't be the president and educate me with this. So all of these years, it's a\u2014 it's a\u2014 these people getting their toes chopped off Amputated?"], [1, "That's not why. If you're getting diabetes, it's usually from sugar. Okay. You know, there's been a lot of misinformation that's spread because of actual scientists that were bribed by the sugar industry. So the sugar industry, they paid a bunch of Harvard scientists. It was Harvard, right? I believe it was. They didn't even give them a lot of money. Was it the 1950s or 1960s, Jamie? Do you remember? So this has all been outed now, but what they did was they tried to associate saturated fat and, and foods with saturated fat with being responsible for heart disease. And they did that to try to get the blame off of sugar, because sugar is fucking terrible for you. It's terrible in basically every way, especially added sugar."], [0, "So why are all of these diseases that we speak of are more, more happening in the black community than in the white community?"], [1, "It's diet. It's diet. It's 100%."], [0, "It's processed food."], [1, "It's processed food. It's diet. It's sugar. It's sugary drinks. It's the amount of sugar. Like, if you say, if you drink like a 1-liter Pepsi, the amount of sugar that is\u2014 okay, let's find out how much sugar is in a 1-liter Pepsi. If you drink several of these a day, one of them, I believe, is more sugar than you're ever supposed to have in a day."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["salt", "iodized salt", "iodine", "high blood pressure", "diabetes", "sugar", "added sugar", "processed food", "sugary drinks", "saturated fat", "heart disease", "diet", "racial health disparities", "food industry", "scientific bribery", "harvard", "public health", "nutrition misinformation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778650997.306375, "duration_sec": 3.893394947052002, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Raiders are in a sticky situation, not because they can't do this cap-wise, but because they just divorced their spouse. Now they've got to take their spouse back. The deals that they have lined up will not be impacted, so they can still do the things that they want to do."], [0, "There's no reason for Max Crosby to be any happier with the Raiders than he was four days ago. There's no reason for the Raiders to be any happier than with Max than they were four days ago. He wanted to be leaving Las Vegas. Dallas finished second, we think, in the Crosby sweepstakes because they didn't want to give up two first-round picks and good for them. Well, won't take two first-round picks anymore. The Cowboys can take advantage here."], [0, "Las Vegas has no leverage. So why would Dallas not, and any other team, any other team that was interested, the Bears or the Seahawks or Rams or whoever else was calling, why would you not try to lowball the Raiders? And here's how you do it. Just 90 hours earlier, you wanted him. Now you don't want him at all?"], [0, "There's no level at which you, the Cowboys, would want Max Carr. Oh, we're out. Aaron Kay, he's not worth a first and second anymore. That's correct. Watch my smoke. Not a first and a second rounder and a player. That was the old offer."], [0, "before doctors got a look at his knee and said, either slam on the brakes, which I don't believe they said, or tap the brakes. Tap the brakes is what I believe the medical determination was. Not he's done with football. So again, we'll just make up, you guys are making up offers and good. You offer them"], [0, "Something lesser and lesser that escalates into something more and more if he plays 12 games this year or 13 or 10 or 17, whatever. You create it yourself. A contingency deal. I called it dog spit on social media. I said, give him a dog spit offer. And of course, they would never go for that."], [0, "Maybe I should have been more specific, but I don't want to be specific because I'm not trying to pretend that I know that here's exactly what they would do or exactly what the Raiders would take. So I'm trying to give examples. Raiders, I will give you a four this year and a two next year. And the two becomes a one if Max plays 12 games, for instance. That's just an example. That's my offer."], [0, "If it's financially helpful, I'll give you a player. If it helps me financially, I don't care about you. If it helps me clear out room financially, I'll give you a player. Just an example. I'm giving you a four this year and a two next year that escalates to a one if Max Crosby plays 12 games. Apple, let's give a second round. You can't give a second round pick this year because you don't have one. I'm giving you a four. That's what I got."], [0, "K-29, Ravens signed Hendrickson. That's why they backed out. That's what I'm saying. Thanks for the pitch into the super chat. The Ravens played trick-em-dick-em here. And by the way, good for them. I'm seeing a lot of people say, nobody will ever deal with the Ravens again. Are you kidding? It's the NFL. The Ravens will survive this."], [0, "To the point of no red shirts, RW. I see a lot of people saying that. Jerry said no red shirts. First of all, he was talking about the draft. And second of all, I repeat, no red shirts, except the Cowboys made an offer on Max Crosby four days ago. So they were interested in a red shirt four days ago at this level. Now simply be interested in him at this level down here."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": 