{"timestamp": 1778655876.381419, "duration_sec": 2.25370717048645, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "you know, behind it all, they were competitors and they just did absolutely terribly. And this was the third Ashes in a row. The teams have done terribly. So, I mean, good on Oli for talking about it. I think in some ways it's actually bolstered our Australian side because we know that the Australian team was very strong now looking at it in hindsight. And since then, the Australian players have gone on to some magnificent wins in T20 and even in some of the larger cups, although they did have one T20 stumble there very early against..."], [0, "in the T20 series there in India, but the Australian side was strong. So it's honestly something we haven't seen before, John, but the English, they're licking their wounds, but they're also looking very closely and, you know, basically being very honest about how they feel about it. Jeremy knows that all week long, my task, my duty, my challenge, my joy for what I do is to report to the national and global audience. I'm national on radio, global on podcasting."], [0, "about the hard news and the news has grown exceedingly dangerously hard these last weeks certainly these last years since the Russian invasion of Ukraine and the Hamas attack on Israel and now the Israeli and American offensive against the suspect nuclear weapons program in Japan very hard news"], [0, "You expect worse every morning as it escalates. And all stories become one big one. So I stay away from it when Jeremy and I talk. I do it all week long. There's no reason to be despairing on Saturday night when I'm asked at the dinner table, what's the news today? I try to talk about anything but the war. It's just"], [0, "Human nature. However, I must now talk about the war. I'm drafted to it because who knew the war and cricket combined to make a cricket ball? Who knew this? Who knew that the jam up at the Strait of Hormuz is going to affect cricket? No, no, it's real. Jeremy, did you know this about the cricket ball?"], [0, "I genuinely didn't know this, John, and this is very, very interesting because what has come out now that's affecting the English teams more than anywhere else because at this time of year, this is when the English teams start to practice in earnest and get ready really for the Northern Hemisphere cricket season and they need cricket balls. But it turns out that the official cricket balls for any \u2013"], [0, "cricket game played around the world are first put together in asia which is effectively the rubber and the cork and the the interior part and the stitching but they then have to be moved to the middle east for the final process which is the tanning and the hardening so i never realized that when i've been picking up cricket balls and this has gone on very much for pretty much since cricket balls were invented in the modern era"], [0, "they actually are made in two places, Asia and the Middle East, with the Middle East hardening, the final part that makes the cricket ball up to specification and the part that makes it up to being usable in any competition, which means that now, of course, because you can't move commodities from Asia to the Middle East,"], [0, "there are no new cricket balls being made, which means at the moment there is actually looming a cricket ball shortage to the point where in England, cricket teams, and I can't believe I'm saying this, are actually having to ration the issuance of cricket balls. They're only giving any cricket side in the English state sides and the English district sides 50% of their quota of cricket balls, which means, John,"], [0, "they predictably will only be able to play maybe about 50% to 75% of the games without having to reuse cricket balls that are just not up to specifications. So cricket itself could actually be under threat this coming year because of the Strait of Hormuz being shut and the fact that we won't be able to actually get cricket balls, which means for Australia next summer, we too may actually see our cricket season clipped and cut short just to ration the cricket balls we currently have on stock."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cricket", "ashes series", "australia team", "england team", "t20 cricket", "cricket season", "cricket balls", "equipment shortage", "rationing supplies", "asia manufacturing", "middle east processing", "strait of hormuz", "supply chain", "global conflict", "war reporting", "international trade", "sports economics", "crisis management"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
