joyed this episode, we'd really appreciate it if you'd give us a rating on whichever podcast app you're using. And remember to subscribe to keep up with each episode. The Tech Optimist welcomes any questions, comments, or segment suggestions. So please email us at info at techoptimist.vc with any of those. And be sure to visit our website at av.vc. As always, keep building."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["deep technology", "science fiction", "supercomputers", "engineering", "technological progress", "innovation", "human history", "aviation", "air travel", "time horizons", "world war one", "tech optimism", "venture capital", "podcasting", "communication", "futurism", "problem solving", "societal change"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778665603.8914018, "duration_sec": 2.284511089324951, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "Mike, do you know with this one that's already permitted what the cooling plan is? And I'm asking this in, in terms of water use, right? Because there are some that have closed systems where, you know, you fill them up with some amount of water but you don't need to use much going on. There's others that use more evaporative processes that use a lot of water all the time. Do you know?"], [4, "As always, Ross, you're asking exactly the right questions, and that's what we\u2014 I have not yet seen the plans for that site. I did visit their current site. They do have a current site that's actually in downtown Denver in the gas and light building. You'd walk by and never know that inside that is a data center. And so that's the things we want to learn and set expectations about is what is your plan for cooling? What is your plan for noise control? What is your plan for water usage? Is it, as you said, a closed loop where you can recycle the same water each day? If so, great, far less impact. And so I have not toured yet that site to see what it is. I have been in talks with CoreSight, but those are the kind of regulations we want to put in place and the conversations we want to have with them on, you know, what innovative things exist in the market that can reduce the water demand, that can reduce the electricity demand. We know they're working on innovating in that way too."], [5, "Pivoting over to the news of Denver transitioning from the Flock safety cameras to Axon for its license plate reader cameras. What makes this company different than what we see with Flock? I mean, we had a critic, a great audio cut that we played a couple days ago of a critic that said, okay, I feel like this is if I said I was going on a diet and I chose that I'm just going to switch from cake to sticky buns. So what is really the difference of what you see in the regulations and the surveillance of these companies?"], [4, "Yeah, there are significant differences. The first one is they have the single highest level of data security of any company in the country. They are the gold standard. Evidence is the database they run. That is where the data for every single body-worn camera of a Denver police officer is stored. It has what's called FedRAMP High, which is the same standard for data security that you have for your medical records. So, like, it is the single highest level of security. So, that's critically important. It also is Denver-only data. It doesn't have a national search database. It doesn't have 1,000 other cities that use it. This would only be used for Denver by Denver police officers. And so, it means no access for federal agents, federal officers, others. And so, and it's a trusted company that we've used in partnership. You know, body-worn cameras are what we use to both, you know, keep the public safe and to hold officers accountable. It's been a real tool for accountability people trust. So we think that same database should be one that we can trust to store license plate photos. And I think that's the key here. We're not using it to train AI. There's no, you know, photos of people's faces. This is bumpers and license plates. And we think that is a reasonable way to still preserve public safety and protect privacy."], [3, "Mike, we have just a few seconds left with you. I want to go back to the main sort of news topic with your executive order about ICE. What do you know? What can you tell us about the level of ICE activity in Denver right now? It's obviously not Minneapolis, but maybe, you know, maybe there's actually a lot going on, but it's just being done in a different way so it's not so visible, or maybe there's not very much. What do you know?"], [4, "[Speaker:ADAM] We have not seen high-profile, large-scale raids like the sort that happened a year ago when they first arrived, or like what you've seen in other cities. We think there are ongoing day-to-day operations, but we've not seen big upticks in that activity, and we'd be delighted to not see that, and we don't think there is a reason for it, and we think that is because we have worked so hard as the Denver Police Department to identify, pursue, arrest, charge any violent criminals in the city. I think the notion that if ICE is prioritizing violent criminals, they're not going to find them in Denver because we're working hard to find them already, sometimes in partnership with the federal government. And so, so I think that's why there's not a reason for them to come to Denver. We have the largest drop in crime in any big city. We think we're handling things well now, but we want to keep it that way."], [5, "Denver Mayor Mike Johnson, appreciate your time as always. Just want to let you know, a texter did say that you only steal street signs, you don't steal grapes. Borrowed street signs, I guess, is what you said. Thank you so much for your time."], [4, "Thanks, Kel."], [0, "No one knows what the future holds, but you deserve a weather app that can help. WeatherBug is easy to use and provides forecasts for your every need, from storm warnings to pollen levels, right at your fingertips. Get the fastest local alerts and comprehensive 10-day forecasts wherever you are. Its hyperlocal, real-time, customizable alerts make sure the weather never takes you by surprise so you can plan every day with confidence. Download the free WeatherBug app from the App Store today and start getting accurate weather forecasts 24/7. Ah."], [1, "Sink into affordable luxury. Annabae is the only mac