s\nAs e-commerce reshapes shopping habits, malls across the Philadelphia region are rethinking their tenant strategies by integrating unique, experience -focused retailers that encourage in-person visits."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["city of seattle", "seattle city council", "libraries education and neighborhoods committee", "seattle city hall", "sound transit", "2026 adopted budget", "2026 adopted budget and financial plan", "city budget office", "mayor bruce harrell", "government finance officers association", "distinguished budget presentation award", "councilmember maritza rivera", "joy hollingsworth", "dionne foster", "eddie lin", "alexis mercedes rinck", "quynh pham", "department of neighborhoods", "seattle washington", "philadelphia"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778669806.5009024, "duration_sec": 2.107999563217163, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "into something and they're like, well, I don't want to bother them right now. So I'll just wait till later and ask if they can pair. I don't want to disrupt their flow right now. And then when we move to this remote world, you don't, you can't even tell anymore. Like, you know, it's this weird thing where like, is everybody really, really busy right now? Or if I'm, if I'm just going to disrupt them. So we were like, oh no, no, no. We don't want like review to ask that other developer. Hey, can I get your help? They'd be like, they could, they would tell you like,"], [0, "I can't right now, but maybe in a half hour or something like that, I'll do it then. But because they looked really busy and focused. And then on the flip side, we could see when an intern or a junior developer or anyone on the team was kind of like struggling, you would notice that they were like, right. Overflow for every time you glance at the screen, you need to talk about anything. Yeah."], [0, "So, but when it's remote, I don't know how teams are doing that without proactively doing it because if someone's asking for help, I'm just going to assume that they've already hit their head against the wall for a while. Got the courage to ask and like, hey, I need some help over here."], [0, "Yeah, no, that is really interesting observation about the like pre pandemic life. And yeah, again, like with my own consulting business, I've worked very solo and always remote. So I haven't had that experience. But yeah, I think that is one of the nice things about having remote work where you can't necessarily see what other people are working on. It's easy to just be on Slack, like, hey, I need some help. Is anybody around to pair with me? That's really nice, I think."], [0, "But you're right. The flip side of not being able to tell if somebody is somebody is stuck or not. Sometimes I think, well, I know sometimes I feel like you just have that little like, oh, this person's been kind of quiet. Let me reach out and see if they are stuck or if they need any help on anything."], [0, "I just wonder how difficult it is for a junior developer coming up in this environment now. And they're probably learning a lot of other skills. I grew up in my career around people in the development team. I'd be able to ask people to help or slide over my chair and be like, hey, can you help me or slide over here for a moment?"], [0, "which felt quicker than like, hey, do you have time to do this? And like, yeah, let's schedule a thing, put it on the calendar, or let's pop into Tuple right now. We'll do some pairing right now. It kind of varies. Or there's also the Slack conversations back and forth in the big thread where three or four developers are kind of trying to figure out how to debug something, which is great too. And there's another part of me that's like, can we all just hop on the pairing tool real quick and just see if we can work this out in the code? Because did you try that? And then you're waiting for five minutes for them to let you know. Oh, right. Yeah."], [0, "They're typing. You're like, did it work? What happened? Or is this solved yet? So it's an interesting different sort of communication challenges that I think teams are needing to navigate, come up with their own conventions for communicating that with each other. While preparing for this discussion, you mentioned that you've been a total convert to non-dry tests. Oh, yeah. Tell me more about this."], [0, "You know, I really like to write dry code as much as possible and, you know, just sort of like organizational kind of personality. So, yeah, writing tests, it's like, OK, I'm going to like meet this up and we're going to, you know, we use our spec generally for my projects. And, you know, I'm going to use let statements. And but anyway, so that was generally how that was how I learned to write."], [0, "tests and then I wrote them for years but then I did start to get to the point where something would break and I would go back and I'm like like code that I had not looked at for quite a long time and it's felt like a slog like oh my gosh I'm like how is this variable being defined I'd have shared tests and like different files and it's like oh I'm spending a lot of time you know kind of trying to go back and figure out how these variables are defined and then I was doing I guess maybe there was"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["remote work", "pair programming", "developer collaboration", "asking for help", "junior developers", "mentoring", "slack communication", "calendar scheduling", "debugging", "communication challenges", "software testing", "dry code", "consulting business", "productivity", "knowledge sharing", "code readability", "test maintenance", "developer workflow"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778670334.9444838, "duration_sec": 2.259744644165039, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Welcome to a special bonus episode of Rework. On the last episode, I sat down with Jason Free to talk about selling. We focused on this water selling challenge he had seen a few years back, and that led to a question I had about Basecamp's semi-recent price change. It didn't really fit into the full episode, but I thought his answer was interesting enough to share anyway. So I started by asking Jason if he noticed any trends in the four companies who actually turned a profit selling water."], [0, "Well, I did notice that they typically, I think if I remember correctly, they had low inventory levels. So they bought limited amounts of water. And I think most of them sold out of their full inventory. Yeah, I believe three out of four of them ended up with zero inventory again. So that means they may have priced too low. They may be able to make more money. They may have, whatever. But all that being said, I think it's cool because..."], [0, "While we can look at that and go, well, maybe they sold it too cheap and maybe they, whatever, they still came out with a profit. So they did fine. Like this idea, like they didn't, you know, maybe they left money on the table or whatever, but who cares? They came out with a profit. That was a good lesson for them. Yeah. So Basecamp recently decided to change our pricing model. Is that based on the same concept? Selling water. Are we selling our water too low? Too cheap? Well..."], [0, "I think so. Yeah, I think so. There's a variety of things that we looked at there. So we actually did some competitive price analysis on, I think it was like 25 different products that are roughly in the same space. Some compete directly, some don't, but they're kind of in the same area. And it turns out that Basecamp's"], [0, "For Basecamp's average user, Basecamp was the cheapest product or the lowest cost out of all 25 or whatever it was. And that actually bugged me. I was like annoyed by that. I'm like, you know, if the only reason we've been successful is because we're the cheapest, that like, I don't know if that was true, but it was like that entered my mind. I did not like the sound of that. So that was where it started. And then we also felt like, you know, Basecamp's more valuable than 29 bucks a month, which is what we were charging at the time."], [0, "um and i talked to a variety of business owners recently who who i was talking about base camp they're like how much is it i'm like 29 bucks a month they're like that's it like how per person right i'm like no no that's it like 29 bucks a month period and they're like i don't think i would trust something that's that cheap to run my business on you know so there's a variety of things that were adding up and ultimately um"], [0, "So we just decided to take a swing at something, which is like, let's get rid of all the tiers. We had like 29, 79. I think there was like 129 or 115. We'd played with a variety of tiers. Over the past five years, we'd had like five or six tiers. Yeah."], [0, "And we said, let's get rid of all of them. Let's raise the price to $99 a month flat still. And let's signal some seriousness in the product. Let's also say it's absolutely worth this. It's still, I believe, a great bargain. I think it's a steal, actually. And let's see what happens. And we didn't change our prices on our existing customers, which was the key. So we didn't \u2013 anyone who was paying us 120,000 customers or whatever \u2013"], [0, "In total, they're still paying us the original rates. So this was just for new customers. It was a test, basically. And it turns out that signups fell, as we expected, but only by a little bit. But average ticket price per sale went up significantly, and conversion rates went up as well. And so it was a huge success. And so it's kind of a cool thing to say, like, we can raise our prices three times and actually have a better business after the fact. Yeah."], [0, "And doing that while, and we were already profitable, right? So it's not like we were doing that to try to fix a problem."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["pricing model", "price increase", "basecamp", "selling water", "profit margins", "inventory management", "price analysis", "competitive comparison", "product value", "subscription plans", "customer signups", "conversion rates", "saas industry", "market positioning", "business strategy", "customer trust", "revenue optimization", "experimentation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
