{"timestamp": 1778671832.7156749, "duration_sec": 2.614328145980835, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "Gladstone started to tell about Algodones. He says, \"Why don't you move to Algodones? Because Algodones is 4 minutes from Yuma.\" He says, \"If you go there, you're going to have a lot more patients.\"."], [1, "At first, Maga\u00f1a was skeptical. He paid Los Algodones a visit, and it was a lot like he expected. A sleepy little place with a lot of mostly drunk people. But many of those drunk people were Americans. Its proximity to the border meant there was already a culture of coming and going. He saw the Americans spending their money at the bars and thought maybe they'd spend money on dentistry too. Maga\u00f1a knew he could offer them a price lower than what they could get in America."], [2, "And my brother-in-law, he got the idea and says, I'm going to put an office in Algodones. And he put the office, and he grow so fast. And he has so many patients, so many patients."], [1, "Maga\u00f1a set up his clinic right across the street from the border crossing. On his first day, he saw 9 patients. And for the first 3 years, he was the town's only dentist, sometimes working from 6 in the morning until 11 at night. The demand for health services ended up being so much more than he could handle on his own. So he started asking others to come set up shop, including Dr. Medina."], [2, "And then I have the idea to come and practice right here."], [1, "Maga\u00f1a helped Medina acquire a small office for his practice."], [2, "He give me a key. He says, \"Take this.\" \"What?\" \"Go to my office and see.\" And then I come and I see he made me a very small office, you know, 2 rooms, waiting room and the office to give attention. A table, a desk. And that was my first, my first practice office I have, you know."], [1, "Medina remembers those early days with amazement. Once he stepped out of his office to see a line of people, Americans, all waiting for an appointment with him. He wasn't even taking appointments. He says the demand was immediate."], [2, "And I start to work and I start to see American people, you know, hard time because I don't speak too much English, but I don't kill nobody, you know."], [1, "Maga\u00f1a and Medina were treating patients who would talk about their struggles with healthcare in America. For most Americans, dental care has always been out of reach. Early dentistry was considered more of a craftsman's trade. Dental work was performed by barbers and blacksmiths, people who were considered quacks by the larger medical field. And ever since then, dentistry and general medicine have fought hard to keep themselves separate. Cut to today, and dental and healthcare are still separated. They have separate insurances, separate teaching schools, separate medical records, even. It's as if matters of the mouth are separate from the body. But of course, they're not. Every year, hundreds of thousands of Americans end up in emergency rooms for something like a toothache, toothache, something that could have been treated in a dental office. But most ERs don't have on-call dentists, so often these patients are sent home with painkillers and are told to go visit their dentist. But a lot of these people don't have dentists, or if they do, the cost for a visit is impossibly high. And yet people are held personally accountable for the state of their teeth in ways that they're not held accountable for many other health conditions. Poor dental care can be both life-threatening and humiliating, as if unhealthy teeth are a failure of individual responsibility rather than the symptom of a broken system. In Algodones, Dr. Maga\u00f1a saw the state of dental care in America as an opportunity to turn this small Mexican border town into a place full of dental services catered to desperate Americans and Canadians. Looking for deep discounts. But the town was still mostly strip clubs and bars, and he felt like that was holding them back. So in 1980, Maga\u00f1a became mayor, although the technically accurate term is municipal delegate. As municipal delegate, Maga\u00f1a shuttered most of the cantinas. It was his first major step in transforming Algodones into Molar City. Year after year, the same buildings that housed those cantinas gave way to more clinics and pharmacies."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["dentistry", "dental clinics", "border town", "los algodones", "yuma arizona", "american patients", "dental tourism", "affordable care", "healthcare costs", "dental insurance", "emergency rooms", "municipal government", "mexican economy", "language barriers", "medical history", "cross border travel", "public health", "regulation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778672658.8516402, "duration_sec": 2.813122510910034, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "different words and the different phonemes in those words and adjust some of these expressive features emotion and pitch and mixing every mixing different voices together as well like to create a mix of synthesized voices you can do all this within this kind of advanced editor which seems seems really possible or uh really powerful is what the word i was looking for"], [0, "I'm really looking forward to using it, but I'm a little dismayed that I'm currently in number 6,466 in line to receive it. So it may be a little while before I receive the joys. Well, shout out to Josh. If you're out there listening, you can bump Chris up the waitlist."], [0, "But yeah, I think it's really interesting where, you know, it's one thing to like produce a synthesized voice. It's another thing to kind of have multiple voices, maybe in a video that you're mixing down and mix voices together, change the expressive qualities like."], [0, "It's sort of almost like working with synthesized voices like people do with like computer production of music, right? Where you can change things and mix things together and all of that very fluidly."], [0, "I want to ask you a question that's very specific to the work that you're doing on a day-to-day basis. And for working with indigenous populations and their languages and stuff, what are some of the ways that you think that this will change that going forward or add to it that you guys have been talking about? What's the future look like for someone in your line of work on that? I'm just curious about that kind of a real world aspect."], [0, "Well, yeah, I think that there's definitely the side of this, which is probably the more commercial side of it, which is, you know, media production and that sort of thing where like, let's say that you produce a video in one language and, you know, you're wanting to do the dubbing across languages or something like"], [0, "that or maybe even you're using like an avatar and using synthesized voices in your video and the whole thing is synthesized I mean that's happening quite a bit right now as well and so the ability to bring in multiple voices and do all that without going into a recording studio of course that has you"], [0, "huge applications for like advertising, marketing, media production, entertainment, all of those different areas, which is where I would guess, and I can't speak to Koki's business model, but I would guess that their tooling is quite applicable across those areas."], [0, "For local language communities, I think, of course, they're also involved oftentimes in the production of media or content for their communities. So that's also relevant there. But I think there's also unique things that are relevant in those scenarios. So imagine that you're part of an indigenous community, a local language community, and you are kind of marginalized by the national government or discriminated against in one way or another."], [0, "It might be a kind of big ask for you to for your community to say, hey, could we put up 100 hours of content with your voice and maybe your likeness? Right. That's potentially painting a target on yourself. Right. Yeah. When you're associating yourself and you're the face of that community. So."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["speech synthesis", "voice editing", "phonemes", "emotion control", "pitch control", "voice mixing", "audio production", "music production", "media production", "video dubbing", "indigenous languages", "marginalized communities", "advertising", "marketing", "entertainment", "avatar technology", "language preservation", "discrimination", "privacy concerns"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778673264.148757, "duration_sec": 2.8676626682281494, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "How are you doing? Hi, Gary. Hey, Gary. Welcome on stage. How has your week in testing been? Oh, it's been, I think, along the lines of what you said, Danny, it's been kind of chaos, but there's been some, I'm going to try and bring the positive vibes into this one because we had a, the main sort of like headline for us this week is that we had a kind of show and tell of one of our product prototypes. And this has been going on for quite a few months when I've been building this."], [0, "And six months ago, like I say, we, or it's about five, six months ago, we put our QA in there because it was being too developer-led. So I was elbowing my way into the room to just say, hey, you could, you could ask us about where it's going and whatever."], [0, "And, and they didn't want to, at the time they were saying things that we don't want to put any delays. We want to get these ideas out. And I said, okay. So I was quite haggling and said, okay, you, when you've got something to show, let us have a look at it. And I was going through the club and that's when I found Simon's link. I can't remember the article, but it was talking about the quick P quip. It was exploratory sort of session. And we put as a result of that."], [0, "a couple of ideas that this product could do. And again, that's to do with mindsets is that we would, I haven't read the article yet, so I don't want to take it too out of context, but, but yeah, we was thinking of what would this type of user do? What would they actually, what would be their main business benefit from this, regardless of what they think is going to be the business benefit. And we put that in there and then"], [0, "We then had a product demo that had those features in it. And so I had to like very tactfully and diplomatically remind them, these ideas actually came from us. So that was really cool that we'd, even if it's like in the back of their minds, made a difference to the product direction, if you like, by just getting involved in there early, which was really cool. But yeah, so that was the main thing for me, but also I was coming up"], [0, "around Demi and Demi's thing about the sort of manager leaving as well or manager having to go. I think it's one of these kind of moments are really potential team bonding moments where everybody in your team is affected."], [0, "So getting everyone together to say, okay, so what's going to be the impact of this? What are we going to miss? And you might actually just get a kind of stronger connection with everyone if you start talking about those things and then saying, okay, who can take on this kind of responsibility? Who can take on this kind of responsibility? And it may actually start bringing people together. And to be honest, if you keep doing stuff like that, Demi, then you'll probably get noticed. And you never know, you might be asked to apply."], [0, "if such an opportunity arises."], [0, "And yeah, so that was one. I didn't want to keep talking, but from Eamon's perspective, it was, I have a lot at work, peaks and troughs of, so at the moment I'm on a peak and I'm really busy, but when you're in the troughs, it's really tough to stay motivated, but you then have to look at those things as opportunities of, okay, what can I do here that will make, make a difference? What can I learn here that's going to get me some time, give me some time back or give me some sort of efficiency?"], [0, "You can always make a difference. And like I said in the comments, it's the thing you have to, because I can get, sometimes I can get punished myself. So I really should know more than this. But if you take a step back and you look to say, I've been in a job not for long. Is that, do I know more than I did yesterday? And am I heading in the right direction? Then you're heading in the right direction. And I do feel is that if you knew everything, then it's time for you to move on."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["software testing", "quality assurance", "product prototypes", "product demo", "exploratory testing", "user needs", "product direction", "stakeholder communication", "diplomacy", "developer collaboration", "team bonding", "motivation", "leadership transition", "career development", "innovation", "business value", "decision making", "continuous learning"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
