{"timestamp": 1778675295.9677129, "duration_sec": 2.3876733779907227, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "President Trump has insisted that this is the top priority for him and he says it should be for lawmakers too. The president at the GOP issues conference yesterday doubled down on a previous vow to not approve any other bills until this becomes law."], [0, "Let's go for the gold and let's just not accept anything else. I'll tell you what, I'm willing to just sort of say, I'm not going to sign anything until this is approved. I really am, but that's how bad it is. If you don't get it, big trouble, my opinion. If you get this, you're midterms and they know it for two reasons. Number one, they won't be able to cheat."], [0, "And number two, the people are demanding it. And theoretically, there is a path forward despite Democrat pushback. One way would be to nuke the 60-vote filibuster, which is what requires Democrat support in the current makeup of the Senate to pass most legislation. But this is unlikely to get Republican support, so Senate Majority Leader John Thune said it's simply not an option."], [0, "there aren't anywhere close to the votes, not even close to nuking the filibuster. And so that idea is something, although it continues to be put out there, is something that doesn't have a future. So is there another way of getting there? We'll see."], [0, "Another pathway forward that's been pushed strongly by President Trump himself, as well as several senators, Senator Mike Lee is the leading force here in calling for a talking filibuster that would look like an unlimited debate instead of the usual 30-hour debate window that's provided for most legislation. It would require Democrats to hold the floor for as long as it takes for debate to wind down"], [0, "before a vote. And if Republicans are able to outlast Democrats in this, they would be able to pass the Save America Act with a simple majority vote instead of needing Democrat support. The idea has gotten a lot of steam online, but Senate Majority Leader John Thune dismissed the online push as the product of a paid influencer ecosystem. Senator Mike Lee last night pushing back. Tens of millions of Americans have jumped on the cause of supporting the Save America Act."], [0, "and using the talking filibuster to advance it. I regret that some in Washington tonight are dismissing all those who feel this way and who have weighed in. Senate Leader Thune insists that the result of invoking a talking filibuster is unclear, but he says he's still talking with the conference on how to move forward. Reporting from Washington, D.C., Melina Weiskub, NTD News."], [0, "A group of Senate Democrats is threatening to shut down Senate business. They're demanding that Secretary of State Marco Rubio, Secretary of War Pete Hegseth, and other senior officials testify on the Iran war. Senators including Cory Booker, Chris Murphy, and Tammy Duckworth say hearings are needed to answer questions on the war's cost, duration, rules of engagement, and civilian casualties."], [0, "Democrats plan to force repeated votes on resolutions to halt U.S. military action using the War Powers Act. Senate Republicans are pushing back. Senator Katie Britt criticized Democrats for playing quote political games amid the ongoing DHS shutdown. Britt cited growing airport delays and unpaid staff."], [0, "BRIT called on Democrats to stop blocking negotiations and focus on national security. Meanwhile, President Trump says the shutdown is slowing down agencies that track threats, like whether Iran has activated sleeper cells in the country. And House Republicans begin their annual policy retreat in Florida. One of their biggest questions, will the GOP push a second reconciliation bill? NTD's Chris Bob has the details."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["us senate", "republican party", "democratic party", "filibuster", "talking filibuster", "senate rules", "save america act", "legislative strategy", "midterm elections", "war powers act", "iran war", "government shutdown", "parliamentary procedure", "influencer marketing", "national security", "airport delays", "congressional hearings", "reconciliation bill"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778675374.8112397, "duration_sec": 3.0993125438690186, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So the most common one is SDL. So SDL is, you know, has things like, you know, add audio track or, you know, draw 2D box, draw sprite, move sprite, things like that. And then under the hood, they've written what that means."], [0, "you know, in a hundred different platforms. So if you draw a box on Windows or if you draw a box on Linux or on the iPhone or on the, you know, Android phone or whatever, there's a line of code that handles that. So SDL, just think of it as like"], [0, "the same drawBox function implemented like 30 different ways so that when you call it, you don't have to think about it. SMFL is similar. It's a little bit more modern, but it's the same idea. There's also this combination of three libraries, OGRE, OIS, and OpenAL."], [0, "Ogre does graphics, OIS does input, and OpenAL does sound. And when you put the three of them together, you have kind of the same thing. There's hacks, which is very similar. And then something that's becoming much more popular now is the whole HTML5 canvas thing, which is pretty cool. So, you know, in canvas, you can say like raw things like draw a circle, move a circle, things like that."], [0, "And then it will sort of do the right thing. So on the iPhone, the HTML5 canvas will call whatever the low-level draw circle function is for the iPhone."], [0, "So, yeah, the low-level ones are cool. I mean, it's pretty clear, like, if you use a low-level, as the name suggests, you're going to be writing a lot more code. I mean, talking about, like, the content creation stuff we mentioned earlier, you're going to be doing all of that from scratch, and it's going to take, you know, months and months. But, you know... That's the fun part. Yeah, it's true. But if you want to make something like, say, Minecraft,"], [0, "which is just very, very different. Minecraft, the world is made up of a billion of those little cubes, and you can't just draw a billion cubes in 16 milliseconds. It just doesn't work. So you have to be really clever about which cubes I draw and stuff like that. And so whenever you need that much precise control, then you have to use a low-level engine."], [0, "And that's why you don't see a lot of really fancy artwork and things like that in Minecraft. Because you can't just take some model that you made in Blender with the animations and everything and just put it in Minecraft and it magically works."], [0, "um yeah if you're gonna do everything you need a disproportionately large team the more you're gonna do right if you're gonna do everything from scratch right um you need more and more people and then more people to handle the the interactions between them yeah exactly so you end up with like 100 person game teams"], [0, "Yeah, can you imagine like a hundred people all working to make one? Is that reasonable? I mean, I feel like I saw something about some of the games now getting to that size."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["sdl library", "sfml library", "game development", "graphics rendering", "audio playback", "input handling", "cross platform", "html5 canvas", "low level engine", "sprite drawing", "performance optimization", "game team size", "ogre engine", "openal audio", "blender modeling", "minecraft example", "software engineering", "project management"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
