{"timestamp": 1778676849.8167655, "duration_sec": 2.2961575984954834, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And perhaps the Republican Party is already retreating. I can't tell. There are a lot of retirements, but that happens every year. Your measure of these numbers, higher oil promised, higher inflation promised, the housing market slowing because people don't want to give up their really good rates, 4% and less, and young people seeking houses don't want to purchase something that costs more than it does to rent."], [0, "Is there a remedy here? Are we looking at a defeat in the midterms and get over it? Good morning to you. Yeah, good morning, John. Yeah, as we've talked about, it's going to be very difficult. Things are starting to get cemented in place as we go forward. The time is running short for the Republican majority and for the president. Obviously, the most immediate thing that can happen is outside the control of Congress. That would be a successful resolution of the Iranian conflict."], [0, "We don't see one on the horizon right now. In fact, what is happening is talk is that this is Iran's version of escalation where they try to close down another choke point to try to create more economic disruption and try to leverage the United States administration into a"], [0, "into either backing off and or accepting Iranian peace terms. That's where we're at right now. There with the House Republicans, they can still try to start working on legislation, handle the domestic agenda, which is what they have largely control over with a majority, and address issues such as health care and try to find ways to help alleviate the burden that Americans feel in their pocketbooks. In the end, as we've talked about, Americans are very practical people."], [0, "They do not feel the palpable benefit of Republican policies impacting them. They will turn for an alternative, and it will be the Democratic Party in November."], [0, "The Democratic Party in the House of Representatives, my reading of what a House majority can do is very limited given that the president is not likely to endorse their decision-making and it's likely to be contested by the Republican minority. So that leads me to believe that there will be no change. There will be change of leadership in the House for what is or is not debated during the week."], [0, "but no change in the prospect of inflation, the price of oil, or the war if it remains in the Gulf. The House cannot do that. There's new prospect of the Senate being in play. Would the combination of the House and the Senate in the hands of the Democratic Party make a material change in inflation, the price of oil, or the war? No, that's one of the great ironies. It's going to be the Democrats. All they have to do is not be Republicans. Right."], [0, "They don't have to offer a really clear, crystalline agenda with details. They just have to say that we're going to be an effective check upon this administration to start the process. That was the genius of Rahm Emanuel's strategy way back in 2006 when they said the Republicans are just rubber stamps for George Bush. The Democrats will hold him accountable."], [0, "that allowed them to run as not being Republicans, to stop an agenda of the president without having to put forward their own at that point in time, which then allowed them to shape the battlefield for 2008 and help to facilitate the election of Barack Obama. What the Democrats will do if they get majorities in the House and or the Senate is to slow the Republican agenda and start to lay the groundwork for the 2028 presidential election."], [0, "And I think if those roles were reversed, I think Republicans would probably do the same thing. That doesn't mean that it's going to help the American people particularly. But the Democrats will argue that it's a slow process unwinding what the Republicans have done. And the public will likely understand that because their number one goal is to stop whatever's happening now because they don't like it."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["republican party", "democratic party", "midterm elections", "house of representatives", "senate majority", "inflation", "oil prices", "housing market", "interest rates", "iran conflict", "gulf war", "health care", "foreign policy", "economic disruption", "election strategy", "political accountability", "legislative agenda", "presidential election"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778676870.9224212, "duration_sec": 5.925950765609741, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "3-2 late in regulation."], [0, "Okay, David, what's your prediction?"], [1, "Yeah, I think something, something similar to Zach, maybe a 4-3, 4-2 type of game. It\u2014 Canada, I think there were some, you know, the Crosby thing I think threw some players off and it threw a lot of things off for this team, but I think you saw the looks on all their faces after that, that Czechy game. It was like, okay, breathe, the scare is over, back now, kind of back to it. And so I, I do think that Canada should feel good about their chances again because they still have the quality, but I think it's going to be a lot closer than, uh, and I think it's gonna be another close matchup. Finland will know how to, how to attack Canada now."], [0, "Yeah, we shall see how it all plays out. How about Team USA? They have a challenge as well in the semifinal round as they go up against the Slovaks. Who do you think has an easier opponent in this case, the US or Canada?"], [3, "I think the US has the easier opponent, but at the same time, The Slovaks have just been the darling of the tournament, right? The underdog. Who expected them to get the bye? Who expected Dalibor Dvorsky and Simone Nemec and Juraj Slivkovsky to be like the faces of this Olympic tournament? I, I think the U.S. is going to be tested here. I mean, they are very obviously the better team on paper. And ultimately, I do worry that the depth just isn't going to be enough for Team Slovakia to overcome. But this is\u2014 look, if you can beat Finland, you can play with anybody, and Slovakia has done that. It's going to be a, a challenge, to say the least, because Team USA is very obviously at this point the second best team left in the tournament."], [1, "I think the US haven't hit their stride yet, and, and I think that if, if they\u2014 now obviously this was like, if for them to go and beat Sweden like they did, I, I think that's a good statement win for them. But I do think that like Slovakia, eventually the magic eventually runs out on a team like this. Like, as good as of a story it's been I, I think they've been playing a little above their head in a way. And look, people are going to come at me if Slovakia finds a way to beat the US, that's fine. I just think that there's only\u2014 it hasn't been as tough of a road for them. Like, yes, they came away and they beat Finland, you know, in that opener pretty good, but, you know, things had to kind of go their way for them to even get into the position, right? They had the goal differential even though they lost to Sweden, the goal differential helped them get to, you know, what would be an easier opponent for to get into the, into the semis, right? So, you know, in a tournament like this, I just think with the way the US defense is, the way they defend, the way that they can throw so many different looks at, at teams, they, they can wor\u2014 they don't have to worry too much other outside of that top of the top players, right, of the Slavkovskys, the, uh, the Dvorskys, things like that's where I think the US has that advantage. But look, I think when a team is on a high like this, you have to find a way to channel as much as you can. And I don't\u2014 I just\u2014 I'm\u2014 the only thing about Slovakia is I don't know if they can continue that emotional run for this long."], [0, "We are going to find out. Two big semifinal games coming up in a couple of days and it should be very exciting. And of course, we will have it all covered for you here on the NHL Squad Show. So make sure you stay with us on our next broadcast. If you never miss an episode of your favorite team's Locked On NHL podcast, well, the Everydayer Club is made for you. You will get ad-free episodes of your favorite team show, plus members-only Discord access and more. So head to the link in the show notes or go to lockedonpodcast.com/everydayer for more information. I'm Gil Martin from Locked On Islanders. I want to thank everybody for watching and listening today. I want to thank David Morrisuti of Locked On Leafs, Zach Berman of Locked On Devils. Have a great day, everybody, and, uh, thanks for watching. The Locked On NHL Squad Show."], [4, "This is the exclusive table with the view. This is your name on the list. This is 3x points on dining with Chase Sapphire Reserve and a $300 dining credit. Chase Sapphire Reserve, now even more rewarding."], [0, "Learn more at chase.com/sapphirereserve."], [3, "Cards issued by JPMorgan Chase Bank, a member FDIC, subject to credit approval."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Sports"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["ice hockey", "nhl podcast", "olympic tournament", "semifinal matchups", "team usa", "team canada", "slovakia underdogs", "finland hockey", "sweden hockey", "game prediction", "goal differential", "defensive strategy", "sports broadcasting", "fan communities", "subscription membership", "digital advertising", "credit cards", "sports journalism"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778677723.4293814, "duration_sec": 3.161764621734619, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Thanks for listening to the Michael K Show podcast. He's been here a long time and he's gonna be here even longer. Catch the show on demand whenever you want. Just subscribe to us wherever you get your podcasts. It's such a beautiful thing when I'm in the studio and I get a chance to lock eyes with, with Don LaGreca, just to see him again. It's beautiful. Now I do want to tell you one thing. The difference between doing my show from 1 to 3 and, um, doing the afternoon show. So I walked into our office before, and there are these two bags from the men's clothing company Faraday. And I asked, you know, Ray Dinahan and Anthony Pusick, hey, what are those? They go, oh, they sent, uh, clothes over for Don Hahn and Peter. And I said, yeah. I said, they, they didn't send anything over for me? No, no, no. Can you believe that? And this was like, um, Jalen Brunson branded All-Star stuff. So you take me off the afternoon show and you take me off the list of giving stuff to\u2014 I mean, I, I'm hurt. The people from Faraday, I want you to know I'm hurt. Is it a way to make it up to me? Of course there is. We could talk about that later. But the fact that you didn't even think about it unless you give it to Don, Han, and Rosenberg\u2014 why? What have I done other than be great at my job, other than give you the fastest 2 hours in sports talk radio? What have I done to be treated so shabbily? I don't get it. I don't deserve it, and I don't understand it. And I'll get to the bottom of it. I'll get to the bottom because I've got some names, people that are involved, that are involved, that decided, no, no, we don't have to give Kay anything. Yeah, okay, let's see how that plays out. Bold strategy, Cotton. See how that works. 1-800-919-3776. Let's go to Chris in Bay Shore. Christopher, what's up?"], [1, "Hey Michael, how you doing?"], [0, "I'm great, how about you?"], [1, "I'm good, thanks. I'm good. Listen, I, I totally get what you're saying, and I agree with you about Clark not being a Marvin Miller, a mover and a shaker and everything else, but I gotta think that the, the, the between the lines kind of thing here and the timing of it is kind of leading you to think that maybe there is something to it, and it can't be good for the players long term. Um, I don't know. I mean, you know, I'm not wishing that to be the case, but I know you kind of talked about a little bit, but I mean, other than that, what could it possibly be?"], [0, "Well, I think what it is, Chris, is that there's this investigation going on which, you know, could end up being embarrassing if they find out stuff that's not good. And I think that he might have stepped down in order not to be a distraction because, you know, what this whole year should be about is uniting the players about the upcoming CBA. Here's what the players have to do. I'm going to give you the secret sauce because that's what I'm here for. They have to stand united, where if the owners are insisting on a cap, the players have to say no if they don't want the cap. Now, it's gonna be awfully hard to convince every single player that the cap doesn't work when they look at their brothers in the NBA and the NFL who are making a truckload of money in a capped sport. Truckload. And I think what the owners are banking on is that there will be a split amongst the players. Because last year, the last collective bargaining agreement where they, they got through the Cohen tax\u2014 it's funny they call it the Cohen tax, it should really be called the Dodger tax\u2014 but they got through the Cohen tax. There are, I think, 6 or 7 players on the executive committee. So these are all big-time players, big money makers, most of them, most of them clients of Scott Boras. So Scott Boras plays a big role in this. So the deal that was ultimately voted on The executive committee went to the rank and file and said, we recommend that you vote no on this proposal. It was ratified overwhelmingly because the players, most of them, don't want to lose paychecks. Some of them support big families. They don't want to lose paychecks, and they're not thinking so far ahead that they're going to affect a player that comes up in 2040. They're not. And that's what makes a strong union\u2014 when it's not about you, it's about others. Will it be strong? And I think that 5 years ago told the owners there's a little bit of a fissure here. There's a little bit of a crack. We could take advantage of it. And I think the owners think, you know what, if they take our final offer back to the rank and file, even if it's a cap, they'll vote yes. Now, I think the big money players will recommend no, but that's not the way it works. The rank and file has to vote. And when they did it last time, the executive committee And Garrett Cole was on that committee, overwhelmingly recommended to the player rank and file, do not ratify this agreement, it's not good. And they ratified it overwhelmingly. Therein lies the thing that probably emboldens the owners. When we got through the Cohen tax last time, now let's get through a cap. Let's really solve this once and for all. Because just think about all the fear that the executive committee had that it was going to hold down salaries. Big secret, it didn't hold them down at all. Salaries have exploded over the last 5 years. Exploded. You see the money that Bo Buschett got? You see the money that Cody Bellinger got? You see the money that, um, Kyle Tucker got? Here would be my advice: don't accept anything that you're diametrically opposed to. And I, again, being a capitalist, I don't believe that anybody should have their earning potential capped. So that's just Michael right now, Michael K. telling you why should anybody be capped. Because if you told the owners, by the way, in your other businesses we're going to cap how much you could make, Steve Cohen's head would explode. Guggenheim Partners' head would explode because in real capitalism you don't cap anything. The market tells you the story. But the owners of baseball could say every other sport has it and they're surviving. Here's my deal, and I said it 5 years ago when I was doing the show with Don and Peter. You put something in place and the owners will climb over dead bodies to circumvent it. So you're putting something in to protect them from themselves and they will circumvent it. The Dodgers have circumvented everything with the deferred money. They, they just circumvented it. Nothing stops owners that want to do things. That's why this time they want a cap, because if it's a hard cap, you can't circumvent it. But a tax? The Dodgers laugh at your tax. Here's your tax. They laugh at it. Has the tax stopped the Mets? No. Has the tax stopped the Yankees? No. Did it stop the Blue Jays this year? No. So if I'm the players, I would know if there's a crack, a little bit of a hole in the agreement, the owners will find it and they'll try to circumvent it. That\u2014 it's one of the great mysteries in all of negotiating. They put stuff in place to stop themselves, and you know what? They cannot stop themselves. They cannot stop themselves. Let's go to Anthony in Toms River. Anthony."], [1, "Hey, Mike, how are you?"], [0, "Good, Ant. What's up, man?"], [1, "Always good talking to you. It's always a learning experience."], [0, "Oh, thanks."], [1, "A good one."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Sports"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["sports talk radio", "podcast subscription", "studio banter", "promotional merchandise", "clothing brand", "baseball union", "player investigation", "collective bargaining", "salary cap", "luxury tax", "player solidarity", "contract negotiation", "sports marketing", "labor relations", "wealth inequality", "capitalism debate", "nba nfl comparison", "owner circumvention"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
