{"timestamp": 1778683734.6294904, "duration_sec": 3.0199317932128906, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "But yeah, that would be awesome. Do you know when or where you're going to be that we can get these stickers? Because I know that that's the only reason people go to conferences. We're going to be at JSConf and also at NodeConf. And also we're going to be at 200 Frank Ogawa Plaza very soon, hopefully, if the lease doesn't fall through. No pressure. Yeah."], [0, "Yeah, what we're actually looking at, we were actually talking about this earlier today because we're getting close to moving into this new space in downtown Oakland. We're kind of thinking about a way that we can very directly support the Node community and the Oakland JavaScript community by having some additional space. Like if you want to drop by and hack with us or something, we will be able to have a little bit of room and hopefully it won't get out of hand."], [0, "That sounds awesome. I have a kind of a change of subject. I know for a while, Nojitsu very generously donated time and resources to maintaining the NPM package registry. And then NPM Inc. kind of started and took it over. Do you want to talk about the transition that happened there?"], [0, "That's a very accurate description, yeah. Actually, it was Iris Couch before it was NoJitsu, and then NoJitsu acquired Iris Couch, and with it acquired the custodianship of the NPM registry. And they found themselves in a position where getting, you know, the way that the NPM registry was structured, with having a single big couch that was doing, not big couch, a single large couch DB that was serving all of the puts and all of the gets,"], [0, "was becoming very, very difficult to maintain as it does. And the approach that worked really well at the start of 2013 no longer worked by the end of 2013. And they were getting into these situations where it was going down and just impossible to come up. The database was getting too many rights to ever be able to compact. The bandwidth and hosting bills for Nojitsu were getting pretty outrageous."], [0, "So it got to a point where basically Nojitsu had to reach out for help. At the time, we were still kind of investigating whether or not it made sense to make a company around this, whether it was even something that we could kind of build a business model around it. I'm not a fan of the sort of traditional dot-com bubble approach where you just raise a bunch of money and then figure out what you're going to do, right? I didn't want to start the fundraising or the VC funding thing until we had some kind of idea of what a business plan would look like."], [0, "You're not going to pivot NPM into a tower defense game, is what you're saying? I said I wasn't going to talk about vaporware, but I make an exception for that."], [0, "Anyway, I mean, we're basically getting crushed by this exponential wave of people coming into NPM and trying to use it and really being excited about it. And so the scale NPM fundraising drive, which actually we did on this podcast, we came and talked about it on here. That basically got us out from under, you know, out from under being crushed by this wave of people who were adopting NPM."], [0, "But, you know, if you sit down and do the math, like even a pretty junior software developer or DevOps person in San Francisco or in New York, you know, in an American city is going to cost a fair bit of money. And so if you want to have, you know, five, six people full time on maintaining the NPM registry and also pay for hosting and bandwidth and all of this other stuff."], [0, "it's 300 grand is not going to actually get you that far. It was enough to get us to where Nojitsu wouldn't have to make the choice to either shut down the NPM registry or nothing. Basically, they had to find some money or turn it off. With that in mind, we did the scale NPM fundraising drive. Charlie Robbins ran that whole show. It was a Nojitsu operation. I worked on figuring out ways to"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["conferences", "stickers", "jsconf", "nodeconf", "oakland", "coworking space", "javascript community", "node community", "npm registry", "couchdb", "scalability", "infrastructure costs", "fundraising", "business model", "venture capital", "open source", "devops", "software engineering"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
