ther softening before any meaningful rebound. Analysts expect vacancies to continue climbing into 2026 , with the most vulnerable assets located in Boston proper and the surrounding suburbs."], [2, "DEVELOPMENT PLAN FOR BOSTON, MASSACHUSETTS\nThe Proponent, as defined herein, intends to develop on tl1e Project Parcel a mixed-use development including office, residential , hotel, retail, service and/or restaurant uses and parking facilities and related uses in multiple buildings on the Project Parcel (the \"Project\"), all as more fully described below, to be integrated with the transit -related improvements of the MBTA and Amtrak on ..."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": null}, "prompt_chain": null, "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["boston", "massachusetts", "commercial real estate", "real estate", "office market", "office space", "laboratory space", "life sciences", "vacancy rate", "leasing", "residential housing", "construction", "urban development", "urban planning", "mixed-use development", "public transit", "transit improvements", "transportation funding", "housing market", "rental demand"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778684857.3135324, "duration_sec": 2.8082940578460693, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "from search are still very healthy and people kept predicting that search was going to get killed very fast we're not seeing that yet so we're going to sort of look at that as well so yeah so let's maybe sean shall we start with just sort of looking at the different platforms and kind of how they have approached this bringing real-time data if we want to call it that into their platforms so maybe i don't know let's start with gpt and claude because"], [0, "But complexity is an interesting one. It's a little bit different in terms of where they started and where they are now. So let's start with OpenAI, HIGPT, and Anthropic Cloud."], [0, "Yeah, I think with ChatGPT, they were, I believe, like either the first or one of the first in kind of consumer facing applications to bring in these like external search services where I asked for something that's real time would be generous, but, you know, something that's like fairly recently happening because it has to show up on the web at some point in order for the parse it. But basically they're doing a web search behind the scenes and then pulling in that additional contextual data."], [0, "so that they can properly contextualize the prompt behind the scenes and generate a response that makes sense. So it's like, you know, tell me about the weather in San Francisco. And it can go and do that web search because it recognizes that it probably doesn't have access to that. And it's, you know, tremendously valuable because you used to get that sort of like, you know, I've only been trained on data up to such and such a date and I can't, you know, comment on these things. Or you'd have to go and, you know, copy and paste that from somewhere and bring it in. And I think one of the things that,"], [0, "We're seeing is with OpenAI and I think with Gemini and Gemini's direct interface or even with Quad and all the different models is trying to bring more and more functionality directly into the application. It's just like over years, Google has done, even in conventional search, more and more to sort of keep you in the search page rather than delivering you externally. And I think..."], [0, "As things, people get so used to like having conversations and sort of just being able to ask for stuff, you know, I think about what does that look like sort of long-term of if you have ChatGPT and suddenly websites, e-commerce sites can all just be tool invocations of some sort, then do I ever need to go to an e-commerce site? Can I just go to kind of this chat interface and"], [0, "and just say like, Hey, I'm interested in buying some shoes and this is what it looks like. And here's the amount of money I have available to spend and blah, blah, blah. And then it can go and actually like behind the scenes converse with that site, pull in the details, render it. And I never actually have to leave the chat interface. It's like,"], [0, "It's almost like the way I think about it is like how WeChat has become this like super app in China where it's it's almost an operating system itself where it has apps within the app and so forth. And you can do everything in terms of your personal financing and everything all within WeChat."], [0, "That's a great example because it became clear that super apps, especially, yes, in China were leading, I'm going to say their internet because, you know, it is a very, very different way of operating the internet inside China. Meta, at least, were trying to do the whole super app thing, you know, trying to blend Facebook and Instagram and WhatsApp. And I think the vision there was to virtually blend them. And of course, people"], [0, "Basically, users push back hard on seeing bits of their data across platform. And so at least for now, things are still relatively segmented. But yeah, I think that's a great way to look at it. So this morning, I don't use GPT as much, but I thought I would just go in and just kind of see what it does like today. One interesting thing was going in and, you know, when you start typing the prompt box, you can choose, you know, is this like research or an image or whatever? But one of them is actually just web search."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["search engines", "chatgpt", "claude", "openai", "anthropic", "gemini", "realtime data", "web search", "external search", "prompt context", "tool invocation", "super apps", "wechat", "ecommerce", "meta", "data privacy", "platform integration", "internet regulation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778685055.6059496, "duration_sec": 2.0044937133789062, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, but my best experience is is with like, I got a chance to train against champions at their place, you know, when they're at their best. And it's not an ego thing. It's just like, I love to be able to test myself. And I mean, because I'm my biggest competition. And so that whole thing about just what the bow means to me is like, thank you for\u2014 making me better, right, by providing me an obstacle. And the higher the\u2014 you know, the better the person, the better I can become. 100%."], [1, "And so I loved it."], [0, "So I've, you know, for years I'm in there with Gokhan Saki and, you know, Maurice Smiths, and, you know, who\u2014 you know, you name it. I\u2014 there's\u2014 I've gotten\u2014 I consider myself one of the luckiest one of the greatest martial artists on the planet because I get to train with so many people. Sometimes at my house, and I've got all these former champions training. Rampage, when he was champion, I'd go to his place. And honestly, the things I brag about is when I get humbled. Because that's when I learn something. My philosophy is I love to be wrong because every time I'm wrong, I learn something. Something. Absolutely. And so, like, some of the best times for me is like, I know when I was, you know, Michael Bisping was getting ready for, to fight Georges St-Pierre, and we were in Thailand. I was like, yeah, you know, let's, let's like mix it up."], [1, "What were you doing in Thailand?"], [0, "Oh, we were doing a movie out there. Oh, wow. But he had to train. He was getting ready for the Georges St-Pierre fight. And so, you know, I was like, yeah, let's, let's do some rounds or whatever. And I got so winded the second round. I'm like, dude, just whoop my ass. I feel so like, like I'm embarrassed."], [1, "I should have been\u2014 was a cardio machine."], [0, "Yes, he was."], [1, "He was a cardio machine."], [0, "I didn't expect that because we were\u2014 we spent all day on a yacht the day before and he was drinking nonstop. I'm a non-drinker, right? I'm like, this guy's gonna, you know, yeah, I'm gonna probably take it easy on him today."], [1, "He is one of the toughest motherfuckers that ever fought in the sport."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "training", "champions", "sparring", "self improvement", "humility", "learning", "philosophy", "competition", "cardio", "thailand", "ufc", "film production", "yacht lifestyle", "alcohol drinking", "physical conditioning", "athletic performance", "travel"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778685377.1214197, "duration_sec": 2.466538190841675, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Hello, and welcome to another episode of The Bike Shed, a weekly podcast from your friends at ThoughtBot about developing great software. I'm Joel Kenville, and today I'm joined by fellow ThoughtBotter, Fritz Meissner. Hello. And together, we're here to share a bit of what we've learned along the way. So Fritz, what's new in your world? Well, I am looking at optimizing a Postgres query. I have a query that runs a lot of the time."], [0, "It's fairly fast, but it needs to be faster. And what I can see is that it's not using the index that I think it should. I can see the index is there. So now I'm trying to create a new index that Postgres will understand. That's interesting. How can you tell that it's not using the index? With an explain plan. So if you say explain, analyze in..."], [0, "your Postgres client, it'll spit back some information about what the query is actually doing, like what it's doing to retrieve data. And I can see that it is using an index, just not the one I want it to use. Do you think the query planner is correct in thinking that not using this index is the fastest way to work?"], [0, "Or do you think this is a case where you as a developer are like, no, it would be faster if you use this index. Please use this index and don't do what you actually are hoping to do. Yeah, I'm pretty sure that it's wrong and I'm right. But the only way to really know is to try. So we'll create the index and then see what happens afterwards. There's no great loss if it doesn't fix the problem. Or at least it'll be sad that it isn't solved, but we'll try something else."], [0, "How often do you run into situations where you think the database query planner is wrong and you're right? This is probably the first time in my career that I've thought that. Okay. Yeah. Because I feel like I always tend to blame myself in those situations. I'm like, well, if the query planner is choosing to not use my index, clearly I'm indexing on the wrong thing here or the data set is too small or something like that. I need to refine my approach. Yeah."], [0, "Yeah, I guess it is pretty bold to assume that all of the knowledge that's baked into Postgres is wrong and my grasp of it is better. What's new in your world? Oh, I've been recently fighting with some tricky unit tests. Yeah. This is an RSpec test, and I'm parsing data from a third-party API that comes in as JSON, and it gets turned into a pretty complex..."], [0, "series of nested active model objects. Yeah. And my assertion on this parser is that it will return, I give it some JSON, I parse it, and then I expect the response to have certain attributes using RSpec's have attributes matcher."], [0, "And the have attributes matcher allows you to say this key or this method on my object should respond to. And then you can give it a raw value, but you can also give it another matcher. So you can say it should be a string in this range or something like that."], [0, "You can also give it another have attributes matcher to sort of arbitrarily nest. And so that is the assertion that I have. It's a big nested have attributes inside of have attributes. And when some of the nested assertions fail,"], [0, "the error message that I get is almost unreadable. There's just a ton of text on the screen. Yeah. It doesn't really tell me where this failed. My object has 30, 40 keys and then like deeply nested things on some of them. Yeah. And so I can't figure out what went wrong. Yeah, absolutely. Because it'll say object one with 40 attributes in multiple levels doesn't match object two with 40 attributes in multiple levels and it'll list all of them."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["postgresql", "sql queries", "query optimization", "database indexing", "query planner", "explain analyze", "performance tuning", "rspec testing", "unit tests", "json parsing", "third party api", "nested objects", "debugging", "ruby on rails", "active model", "test failure messages", "software development", "problem solving"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
