{"timestamp": 1778686890.7620788, "duration_sec": 2.9022605419158936, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "A journalist who would just plop down somewhere new and try to hack it as a freelancer, which was the case with, I think, most of my journalist friends down there, yourself included. And so I feel like it kind of really speaks to something about the risk profile of an entrepreneurial person. And this is something you get into a lot in the book, that question of risk."], [0, "Do you feel like you internalized some notions about risk or kind of developed a higher risk tolerance as a result of kind of your upbringing and seeing your parents always being on their own?"], [0, "No entrepreneur is guaranteed success. No entrepreneur is guaranteed to grow or to innovate or do anything else. But anyone who becomes an entrepreneur is guaranteed two things, freedom and risk. The freedom to build and work and do things in the way that you can do them because you're the only one in charge of it. And so if you decide to build a company, if you decide to freelance or work on your own \u2013"], [0, "you are completely free in how you do that and what you choose to work on. But that freedom is inseparable from the risk. And the risk is you are no longer guaranteed anything aside from that freedom. And that's financially, obviously, but as well as other things, you know, colleagues and benefits and all those other structures are of the things that a job, which I've heard about, but I've never had, will give you are the opposite of that risk."], [0, "Every entrepreneur."], [0, "Whether they're doing something part-time, like my mother and Paula with their clothing sale that they had twice a year, or whether you're starting a company that you fully intend will grow to be a massive corporation, is going to have thousands of people working for it, you make that choice. And you accept that freedom, which is what you're going for. And you have to accept the risk that comes with it. And it never goes away. You learn to deal with it. You learn to manage it. You learn to manage your anxieties."], [0, "But that psychological battle of the entrepreneur and their soul with that risk is the inherent trade-off you have to make for the benefit of the freedom. And I think it's different for different people. The truth is that I was in a privileged position. I came from a family that supported me. I was pretty well off. I went to Argentina because I knew I could get four pesos to the dollar. And if I needed to call my parents and they could send me a thousand bucks to cover rent for two, three months or whatever, like..."], [0, "I was fine. Not everybody has that. And so it's one thing to say, oh, if you really care about it, give it up and do your own thing. There's a reason why so many of the people of that Silicon Valley startup myth are like young white kids who went to Harvard and Stanford."], [0, "Mark Zuckerberg went to an elite private school. He was okay. He could sort of say, yeah, go and fail and do it. A lot of people are like that. The founders of Warby Parker. I mean, it's like, they didn't MBA at Stanford. They didn't MBA at Harvard. They didn't MBA at Wharton. Okay, well, already you're in a top 3% of people. So you've catapulted down to the top 1%. It's not so easy for everyone to take that risk. And so I don't judge people."], [0, "people. And I completely understand someone who's like, look, I would love to do this, but I can't. I have a family to support. I don't have that backing. It's a reason why only 10% of people become entrepreneurs, right? Only 10% of people go to work for themselves because that risk is a hard pill to swallow."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["entrepreneurship", "risk tolerance", "freelancing", "journalism", "startup culture", "freedom", "financial security", "family support", "privileged background", "parents influence", "anxiety management", "career choices", "social inequality", "elite education", "silicon valley", "business innovation", "decision making", "self employment"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778686949.0813677, "duration_sec": 2.076773166656494, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Why wouldn't we do that? And, you know, Ted Cruz is trying to answer whatever. And so now we have a situation where we've taken out most of the leaders in Iran. We've taken out a lot of the regime, a lot of the Ayatollah. It's being reported that potentially the Ayatollah Jr., the Nepo baby, the impotent Nepo baby, cardboard cutout."], [0, "Weekend at Bernie's son is probably in a coma or dead. And that's just to name a few. I mean, the top clerics, the top guys have all been taken out. It's a high turnover rate. And I don't see Tucker..."], [0, "responding to that in the way you'd think as someone who was calling for it just a few months ago. So again, I'm throwing this out there with Joe Kent because what happens in that time frame from 2020 to now that you've completely reversed your stance on this and you don't have Trump derangement syndrome, I don't think."], [0, "Is Israel derangement syndrome a thing? I think that's just called anti-Semitism. Okay, okay, yeah, probably. That's probably a little bit closer. So that's what's going on there. Tulsi Gabbard, though, being questioned about the intel that Trump was getting from the intelligence agencies about Iran. I thought she had a good response to this. This is cut seven."], [0, "Did you brief the president if he starts a war of choice that the likely result would be that Iran would strike adjacent Gulf nations and close the Strait of Hormuz? Did you brief him on those two facts that I think have been consistently the assumptions of the intelligence community?"], [0, "I have not and won't divulge internal conversations. I will say that those of us within the intelligence community continue to provide the president with all of the best objective intelligence available to inform his decisions."], [0, "Yeah, I thought that was a fine answer, but that is the new thing. They like that war of choice. That's what they keep throwing out there. And again, there's a lot of questions here being posed by these senators that I think you can stop right in their tracks and say it's based off a false premise. But beyond that, one of the things I really think is disingenuous is they keep throwing out this question, Taylor, about why was Trump shocked that Iran disrupted the Strait of Hormuz?"], [0, "The cut that I saw from Trump, and I actually was asking, you know what I did? Because I saw the cut on Twitter and I asked Grok about it. I said, Grok, give me the rundown of this. Did Trump say that he was shocked that Iran closed or attacked the Strait of Hormuz? And it said, Trump expressed shock that Iran had closed the Strait of Hormuz, calling it a major escalation, something he didn't expect."], [0, "And so then I kind of pressed on and I said, well, show me the clip because Tulsi said she's not aware of those remarks and I can't see the original clip. And then it showed me that the actual clip, he said he was responding to Iran striking the U.S. Gulf allies and effectively shutting down or disrupting the Strait of Hormuz. And he repeated that nobody, nobody, nobody."], [0, "um saw anticipated the scale of retaliation against neighboring countries but right there that's different saying nobody anticipated that iran was going to attack some of its neighboring countries that are very close allies to it nobody anticipated that those allies were going to want to get on our side of things and really try to take out iran that's different than saying oh we didn't expect them to use the strait of hormuz as leverage"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["iran", "israel", "trump", "intelligence agencies", "war of choice", "strait of hormuz", "gulf nations", "retaliation", "regime change", "ayatollah", "senators", "antisemitism", "middle east politics", "national security", "energy markets", "political rhetoric", "social media", "fact checking"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
