{"timestamp": 1778693587.9982142, "duration_sec": 1.9287083148956299, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, since we booked this recording, Iowa passed a privacy law. Between when we connected and when we had this call, Iowa passed a privacy law and California CPRA finalized regulations, even though that law has been in existence for over 100 days now. And it's just constantly changing. And I like to think of, like, policies, privacy policies in particular, as \u2013"], [0, "kind of like, do you remember when SSL certificates were kind of like a nice thing to have? You get them for e-commerce sites, but beyond that, you're not really like, okay, what's a big deal? But then all of a sudden, Google, I think Chrome changed up the viewing of SSL secure sites. And now when you visit a website that isn't secure, I mean, I don't know about you, but I don't feel secure. I'm like, this is so sketchy. And I think we're going to see the same with privacy. It's going to be a few years from now, but I think ultimately people are going to"], [0, "have the knowledge that companies don't deserve to just take your information and do whatever they want with it, I think they're going to look back and think about the SSL times. We're going to look back at these times and be like, remember when companies used to just harvest your data and do whatever they want with it? Those times are just ending. These regulations are coming and"], [0, "I don't think anyone disagrees that people deserve a right to privacy. I think that's all like pretty, you know, everyone. Yeah. Everyone kind of wants privacy for everyone. I want privacy. But since I live in Illinois, I get nothing. Yeah, that's true. So, I mean, I think it's something to just get on top of because it's just going to get more intense as time goes on."], [0, "So in that regard, I just had this idea of a non-legalese TLDR for privacy policies and terms. Is that a thing people do? Here's a quick recap in plain English. Maybe even using emoji."], [0, "For sure. For people who don't want to read. Yeah, they do that sometimes. You know, you have the privacy label, which used to be part of the which is based on the nutrition facts label that you find in the grocery store or whatever. My problem with those is that they don't actually end up providing any information. So like they'll say, we share your personal data."], [0, "But then the list doesn't include who you share it with. Well, if you share my personal data with your email service provider, cool, that's very expected. If you share my personal data with a data broker, I have problems with that. And then I have to go in and read the entire policy to actually figure out what it is."], [0, "So I think it's a great initiative. I just don't think we're really there yet in terms of creating this shortened privacy policy too long didn't read that actually provides consumers with the necessary information to make an educated decision as to whether or not to share their personal information with that company."], [0, "Well, you've also expressed concern before with like a visual that kind of gave you a high level overview. And then to like view more, you link into that. I feel like it's been a year since we talked about it, but like, could that confuse users? I mean, then you just end up clicking on a thousand links and getting lost and not getting all the information in one place. I'm personally the type of person where."], [0, "I like to get all my information in one place. I don't have to click on a thousand links to understand what's actually going on. And that just takes a lot more time for the consumer to read the policy at the end of the day. So I like it where it's all in one place. I do think the idea of TLDR, I think it's a great idea. I just don't think we really figured out how to get there yet with a way to present all necessary information to the consumer."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["privacy law", "data privacy", "privacy policies", "consumer rights", "personal data", "regulatory changes", "cpra", "iowa law", "terms of service", "plain language", "privacy labels", "user understanding", "data brokers", "ssl certificates", "web security", "user experience", "compliance strategy", "transparency"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778693651.818526, "duration_sec": 2.2540202140808105, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Alright, welcome to episode 16 of the podcast. Going to talk about some security news. So, Hacking Team just got hacked, which is a company overseas that makes..."], [0, "software that basically spies on people. It's pretty shady, which I think is the word that Bruce Schneier used to describe the group. But they basically, it's spy software. That's basically the bottom line. They trojanize stuff, installs on computers, basically used to pick through and find out who could possibly be a dissident or a protester."], [0, "Stuff like that and they sell it to totalitarian type regimes and They just got hacked and I don't know like terabytes worth of content released and"], [0, "And a lot of the content included interactions between them and some of these governments, and of course, some of the lists of which governments they actually sold to. One of the governments who purchased about $775,000 worth was our government, the American government, the FBI. So that shouldn't be too surprising, but still interesting to know."], [0, "And one other interesting thing which Schneier also mentioned and a bunch of other people are talking about is the fact that they had exploits for tons of mobile systems, right? They had a lot of Android stuff. They had stuff for jailbroken iPhones, but they didn't have anything for..."], [0, "iPhones that were not jailbroken. So basically a stock regular security configuration on the iPhone appears to be pretty secure. Obviously, that doesn't mean a whole lot, but it does mean it's probably the most secure platform. Unfortunately, it's not really saying much."], [0, "So that's an interesting story. It continues to unfold, kind of like the Snowden stuff. We learn more as people sift through the documents. Citigroup is creating their own digital currency called Citicoin, which is pretty interesting, kind of an important thing."], [0, "old guard embracing new, right? You've got the old brick and mortar bank types embracing digital currency. It's definitely seems to be an endorsement that there could be something to this. Clinton has been talking really poorly about China in terms of hacking. Basically, she said, you're trying to hack into everything that isn't"], [0, "stationary, basically, or doesn't move, whatever she used, but really interesting that she's coming this strong about it. I hope this would be an indication of how she would react if she were president, but who knows, when someone's in campaign mode, what's actually going to happen. Eric Holder just suggested that Snowden might have had a positive impact in terms of what he revealed, and that"], [0, "they might be open to discussing things with him. Basically it wasn't anything really strong. Otherwise it'd be even bigger news, but it wasn't quite a bit of a, I don't know, a backing up against the hard line that was coming up before where there was like no conversation. You simply surrender period where it's starting to look more like maybe a plea deal type negotiation thing where they acknowledge he did some good,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cybersecurity news", "hacking team", "spyware", "government surveillance", "data breach", "exploit development", "android security", "iphone security", "fbi investigation", "totalitarian regimes", "digital currency", "political hacking", "software industry", "finance sector", "campaign rhetoric", "whistleblowing", "negotiation strategy", "regulation policy"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778693881.3135266, "duration_sec": 1.9266979694366455, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "99% Invisible is supported by Claude from Anthropic. There's a design decision happening right now that most people won't notice until it's too late. AI is about to get ads. You ask a question and the architecture of the response, what gets prioritized, what gets left out, starts being shaped by who paid for placement. Anthropic just committed to keeping Claude ad-free. No sponsored responses, no third-party influence shaping what you receive. Try Claude for free at Claude.ai/99invisible. 99invisible and see why problem solvers choose Claude as their thinking partner."], [1, "Time is valuable."], [0, "That's why Lowe's Blueprint Takeoffs turn blueprints into quotes faster. Bring us your plans and we'll generate itemized material lists to make quoting easier so you can get back to building. Plus, at the Lowe's Pro Desk, you get access to thousands of building materials not sold in store, and when your."], [1, "Order is ready, We'll deliver everything to the job site."], [0, "Improving is easy at Lowe's. This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. The walk begins at the parking lot at the end of the road. The site isn't much to speak of, just a sprawling expanse of asphalt peppered with cars. Over a million people come here every year to embark on a kind of pilgrimage. They're here to cross the border."], [1, "We are getting ready to go to Mexico this morning. Yes, one more time. So we shall see what the rest of the day holds. There are hundreds of videos online documenting this experience. Watch as many as I did and they all start to meld together."], [0, "That's 99 PI producer Lashima Dawn."], [1, "Some people seem nervous in the videos. Others are more excited. Many will turn to their imagined audience behind the camera and speak in a reassuring tone. You see that American flag up there, y'all? This is one of the safest crossings there is. And it's not scary, I promise you. It is not scary. Everyone here is headed to the same town in Mexico. It's called Los Algodones, and it's a place where, for decades, Americans have roamed the streets. They come here in desperate search of something they need, something that's almost impossible to find where they live. They're looking for a dentist, one that they can afford."], [0, "Every day, thousands of people, mostly American, mostly white, and mostly retired, come to this town looking for relief from the turmoil in their mouths. Here, dental care costs up to 80% less than what it might cost in the US. Dental tourism to Los Algodones is so common that many call this place Molar City. Today, there are nearly 1,000 dentists in this 7,000-person town, and 98% of those in the patient's chair traveled here from outside Mexico. Lasha is going to take it from here."], [1, "Watch any one of these YouTube videos, and you'll see how a trip to Molar City is made easy for an American. For decades, this crossing didn't even require a passport. In one video, a retired couple arrives at Arizona's edge. They do a 360, displaying the massive lot where they've just parked. \"We love Mexico,\" they say emphatically, talking to the camera. The camera then pans to show the rust-colored border wall in front of them. That corrugated monstrosity. They marvel. Then a US Border Patrol vehicle starts driving past the screen, on guard, it seems, for anyone trying to cross in the opposite direction. To this, the camera woman speaks again, as if she's talking directly to Border Patrol. \"Get 'em, boys,\" she says. Then they begin their walk into Mexico. In Los Algodones, there are dense clusters of medical offices and a canopy of billboards above them. Vendors are set up on sidewalks where they sell handmade goods and kitschy souvenirs, like this one graphic tee that says, \"Keep calm, you're on the fun side of Trump's wall.\" And then there are the street promoters, people who are stationed at almost every street corner. Each of them competing for superlatives: the best deal, the quickest appointment, the most popular clinic in town. In Molar City, a street promoter is the first person a tourist will encounter."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["ai advertising", "ad free ai", "sponsored responses", "anthropic claude", "design decisions", "border crossing", "mexico travel", "los algodones", "dental tourism", "affordable dentistry", "retired americans", "border wall", "healthcare pricing", "medical offices", "youtube videos", "small business marketing", "immigration policy", "consumer trust"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
