{"timestamp": 1778696645.1232579, "duration_sec": 2.231429100036621, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "you can see all of the cranes around this building, which is obviously a sign that there's lots of construction going on right around us. And it really, really shines a great light on the things happening in the city. And Janice, I'd like to start with you. In your role as Senior Vice President, Clark Construction has done"], [0, "a wide range of projects in the city of Detroit, all kinds of things, schools, infrastructures and that. And from your purview, how would you describe the overall role this kind of construction has played in this region's economic and sort of physical revitalization?"], [0, "Sure. Yeah, I think everybody's pretty familiar with a lot of the activity that's been happening over the last decade in the business district and the sports and entertainment district. But what I'm really proud and happy to start seeing is some investment in health care and educational facilities in the city. We've been a proud partner with the Detroit public school system, rebuilding a lot of the K-12 schools in the neighborhoods, which is fantastic."], [0, "And then if you look around, I think we're up to nearly 12 higher education institutions that have a footprint in the city of Detroit, which is amazing. So we're bringing the education to the citizens of the city of Detroit. And we're a really proud partner of many of those, some developers, University of Michigan and Wayne State University. I think the word we use is synergy, right? You mentioned higher education institutions. And, you know, when I was a kid, Michigan State was always in East Lansing and U of M was always in Ann Arbor."], [0, "But the synergy that's happening down here is really incredible. Let me ask you, looking beyond any single project, what are some of the important themes you see in Clark's work in Metro Detroit in any number of the projects, education or mixed use? What's a theme that drives your company? I'd say there's two recent themes that I've seen recently. One is the communities in Southeast Michigan investing in and supporting communities."], [0, "K-12 education. The bond passage rate in the state and in the Midwest tends to be dipping a little bit, but in Southeast Michigan specifically, we still see a really nice bond passage rate and a really good evidence of communities supporting and investing in education. And it's resulting in higher performing, healthy schools for student success. The second theme that I've seen, which has been a surprise a bit, is kind of the rebooming or booming of education."], [0, "community centers, and recreation centers. It was pretty dormant for quite some time. Post-COVID, it seems like there's this desire from communities, again, to support those kind of projects, to pass bonds, to support them, and then to provide great facilities for communities to come together, to socialize, for kids to have recreational opportunities, things to do outside of school. And right now we're working on two very innovative projects for the city of Pontiac as well as Eastern Washington to provide some great facilities for those folks."], [0, "And as any great construction project goes, behind it is a really good architect. And Ron Syme, Wakely & Associates, how long have you been in business? We're going to celebrate 75 years. 75 years. And it's fair to say that when you started, you were still using that onion skin paper and pencil?"], [0, "No computers, obviously. Right. So as a sponsor of School Talks, as well as Clark, I sincerely appreciate your continued support of our program, which runs on Sundays at three o'clock. But Ron, in your role at Wakely, what kinds of projects are you currently working on, especially in the education or public sector work?"], [0, "We're working on several bond issues, and most of the bond issues we're working on right now is based on infrastructure upgrades. There's not a lot of curriculum upgrades at this point. Most of it is energy efficiency, roofs, things like that. And the term infrastructure, obviously, a lot of that stuff isn't necessarily pretty, right? No."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["construction", "detroit", "economic revitalization", "urban development", "schools", "public education", "higher education", "infrastructure", "bond funding", "community centers", "recreation centers", "architecture", "energy efficiency", "health care facilities", "post covid", "mixed use", "public sector", "regional investment"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778696897.8032832, "duration_sec": 2.2461256980895996, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "There's no question about it. And as we move on toward tonight's game, what do you think the players who\u2014 you state some of their top players, one was in Syracuse last year, another played at Texas Tech. Caleb Wilson has talked openly about wanting to beat the rivals, but he's not even going to be playing tonight. How do you think the players nowadays take to it? Because they may not be as familiar with the rivalry as past players are or were."], [1, "Rivalries are not for players. That's one of the great broad misconceptions of college athletics. Rivalries are for fans. The players see each other, grow up with each other, starting from their earliest days as travel ball players and AAU players and all that. They know each other no matter what team they might play on. You know, they always do the story every year about the Duke and Carolina guys going to the same barbershop in Durham to get their haircuts and hang out with each other. Same with NC State. You know, one of NC State's players, Allen Lublin, played at Carolina last year. It's, it's not for them. It's for 14-year-old me growing up in western North Carolina, not wanting to go to school the next morning after Dudley Bradley stole the ball away from Clyde Austin and went down and scored a game-winning dunk. It's for my kids who grew up with the opportunity to watch NC State beat North Carolina 2 years ago to win the ACC Championship. It's for everybody who has a neighbor that wears a damn light blue tie or a bright red blazer when they see each other on Sunday. It's not about the players, it's about the fans who support the team."], [0, "And that's not dropping off a bit because fans are still going out to the Lenovo Center, I think more than ever now, more than they were under Sidney Lowe, more than they were under Herb Syndac, more than even Kevin Keats who got them to a Final Four. I think they really feel a sense of familiarity with Will Wade and I think that's going to carry on with time. It's always great hearing the opinions of Tim Peeler and also his extensive knowledge of NC State history and his love of college basketball. I know he'll be tuned in tonight when the Tar Heels and the Wolfpack face off. Tim, it's great to talk to you. Thanks so much."], [1, "Thanks a bunch and everybody enjoy the game."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["college basketball", "nc state", "north carolina", "duke", "acc", "rivalry", "fans", "players", "game preview", "travel ball", "aau", "final four", "sports culture", "community identity", "team loyalty", "coaching changes", "attendance trends", "tradition"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
