{"timestamp": 1778697950.7330248, "duration_sec": 2.1521708965301514, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Because they are better in terms of productivity, usability, resource consumption. They run on many things. So they are getting more common. And on my socials, I actually try to show that to people by sharing the Tui tools that I came across. And yeah, people are just loving it. And we're just happy to see more Tui tools coming up in the recent days."], [0, "I mean, they're a little more portable too in that aspect, right? Because I suppose you don't really necessarily need a GPU to run this kind of stuff, right? You're just kind of running off the terminal, just using characters now. Yeah, exactly. You can run to his own servers. You can even like run to his own like true SSH connection as well."], [0, "yeah there's a pretty good example of that there's one multiplayer game where you just ssh through some server just you know connects you with another player and you can play it on the terminal and everything is running through ssh so it's possible can you use gratitude to like recreate that whole star wars over telnet thing or i suppose you could right"], [0, "Yeah, you would probably do that. It would be just better running faster probably. Yeah, but that would take a long time to do. Yeah. I'm just kind of curious how long it took that guy to do it. For Tui development right now, I'm just kind of curious about, is there any tools if you design something with like a picture that you can convert that into text because you have like the ASCII art thing, right? Is there something similar for Ratatouille that if you had some design that you created that you can..."], [0, "spit that out into something or maybe not possible because that's more like really just creating art with characters rather than kind of going the other way. It's possible. I actually had a recent project where I wanted to have like a splash screen in the terminal. When you run something, I want to like show an image and just wait for a couple of seconds. So I made this happen with Ratatouille. I used the canvas widget and canvas widget makes you able to"], [0, "draw anything on the terminal with some coordinates so i take this image and i convert it to like the color coordinates and i just draw it with ratatouille and you have pretty much graphics it's called ratatouille splash screen and there are like other examples of that too you can even use like nc escape codes to draw something as well and there are like other examples too so it's possible yes okay interesting"], [0, "Now for Ratatouille, it seems quite popular. And you did mention like some competitors, right? We were talking before the show about Bubble T, right? For Go. How feature pair are they? Like how similar are they? Can they both do exactly the same thing? And just obviously different languages, different ways, because Bubble T is basically, you know, Ratatouille, but for Golang, right?"], [0, "yes and no and i don't want to like talk about them as a competitor shout out to them they're just doing great work and i think they are more like high level rather than ratatouille because ratatouille is like like i said we have this back end and we use that to draw widgets but bubble tea i think it provides more boilerplate and makes you able to do stuff more quickly and you know when you're doing things in rust it might take more time and like more effort"], [0, "So they're like comparisons between us and them. And on some aspects, we look up to them, try to take some pages from their book, try to apply different approaches. So yeah, I need t