{"timestamp": 1778700733.6328063, "duration_sec": 2.0569777488708496, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Monday, Monday, Monday, open wide dev fans. Get ready to stuff your face with JavaScript, CSS, Node modules, barbecue tips, get workflows, breakdancing, soft skills, web development, the hastiest, the craziest, the tastiest web development treats. Coming in hot, here is Wes, Barracuda, Boss, and Scott, El Toro Loco, Tolinsky."], [0, "Welcome to Syntax. In this Monday, hasty treat, we're going to be talking about TypeScript, talking a little bit about the differences between using compilers and build tools, and whether or not you still need to use Webpack or Babel with your TypeScript, along with some of the interesting newer options. My name is Scott Talinsky. I am a full-stack developer from Denver, Colorado. And with me, as always, is Wes Boss."], [0, "Hey, I'm excited for the show because these are the questions I had when I got into TypeScript. I'm like, do you still need Babel, Webpack, Parcel, Snowpack? Like, do you still need these things or not? Or like, where does TypeScript compiler fit into it? So we're going to attempt to answer that today. Our whole platform is built on this stuff left and right. So I got lots of thoughts here. Yeah, I thought like this is all fresh in your mind. So I thought it's a good time to do it. Fresh as can be. And both of our sponsors today are also fresh as can be."], [0, "I'm talking about Sanity at Sanity.io and Sentry. Now, Sentry is the error and exception handling tool that is one of our favorites over here at Syntax and a piece of software that I've been using for a very long time myself. In fact, when we launched our new Level Up Tutorials platform built on many of these things that we're going to be talking about in this episode, well, we wouldn't have even dreamed of doing it without Sentry anymore."], [0, "So if you want to get the best error and exception handling tracking service on the market in your app, performance tracking and release tracking and GitHub issue integrating, all sorts of stuff and more, head on over to century.io, sign up, use the coupon code tastetreat, all lowercase, all one word, you'll get two months for free."], [0, "We're also sponsored by Sanity. Sanity.io is the structured content CMS. It's an awesome CMS. Users love actually using it. And I'm going to highlight one feature of it today, which is they have an entire image pipeline. So it will do all of your resizing and compression and all that on the fly. And they just rolled out support for Next Image. So if you're using Next.js..."], [0, "And their new image component, they rolled out support for that. So what that means is that you can do all of your compression and resizing. And it actually happens on Sanity servers. And it also happens on demand, which is really, really nice. So you don't have any like really long build times to wait for when you're building your website. So I'm excited to see that. They just keep rolling out all the awesome stuff. So check it out. Sanity.io forward slash syntax."], [0, "And that's going to get you double the free usage tier when you build your next site. Thanks so much to Sandy for sponsoring. Super. So let's get into it here. Let's talk about TypeScript. And I have our platform is built on TypeScript. I know, Wes, you've been working in TypeScript quite a bit. And one of the things that definitely mystified me when we first started learning TypeScript or converting over, because my platform used to be in Meteor. Okay. Why don't we get TypeScript in Meteor? This was at the time. And people say, oh, you could just use it from Babel."], [0, "And then other people would say, well, that doesn't give you all of the same benefits, blah, blah, blah. And then once we did get it in Meteor, I kind of started to get the understand the difference between the TypeScript compiler and Babel. So to add like the very brief overview, do you want to say like what the TypeScript compiler is and what it does?"], [0, "Yeah, so the TypeScript compiler, the two main things is that it analyzes your code and does what is called type checking. So when you write your code, it will ensure that all of the types that you write, all the functions that you write with passing in arguments, all the values that are returned from your functions, all your variables that are created, all your arrays that are filtered..."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["typescript", "javascript", "css", "node modules", "web development", "build tools", "typescript compiler", "webpack", "babel", "parcel", "snowpack", "error tracking", "content management", "nextjs", "image optimization", "software engineering", "performance monitoring", "developer workflow"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778700778.9704037, "duration_sec": 4.727232217788696, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "City of Oakland Office of the City Clerk\nOakland City Hall,\n1 Frank H. Ogawa Plaza,\nRoom 201\nMeeting Minutes - FINAL\nOakland, California 94612\n* Concurrent Meeting of the Oakland\nRedevelopment Successor Agency\nand the City Council\nTuesday, February 17, 2026 3:30 PM City Council Chamber, 3rd Floor\nThe Concurrent Meeting of the Oakland City Council and Successor Redevelopment\nAgency Convened At 3:31 P.M., With Council President Jenkins Presiding As\nChairperson.\nCouncilmember Janani Ramachandran Participated Via Teleconference Pursuant To The\nBrown Act\n1 Call To Order\n2 ROLL CALL / CITY COUNCIL\nPresent 6 - Rowena Brown, Noel Gallo, Janani Ramachandran, Zac Unger, Charlene Wang, and\nKevin Jenkins\nExcused 2 - Carroll Fife, and Ken Houston\n3 Modifications to the Agenda and Procedural Items, including but not limited to\nRequests To: Reschedule Items From Consent To Non-Consent, items To The Next\nCouncil Agenda, Speak On Consent Calendar, Register Votes, Change Order Of\nItems, Reconsiderations"], [0, "How Local Zoning Changes Are Shaping Cannabis Retail Locations\nThe shifting landscape of local zoning laws is having more influence on cannabis retail than many might notice at first glance. Across urban and suburban areas, rules that determine where dispensaries can operate are being reconsidered, tested, and adjusted. These local decisions matter a great deal because they alter not only the commercial footprint of cannabis but how communities experience ..."], [1, "Department of Cannabis Control\nIf you intend to celebrate the unofficial cannabis holiday on Monday, 4/20, the California Department of Cannabis Control (DCC) has a simple reminder: keep safety in mind and purchase from only licensed cannabis retailers."], [2, "Oakland Cannabis Dispensary Laws \u2014 Unique Rules\nOakland 's distinctive cannabis dispensary regulations: the first equity program, Measure Z, cannabis taxes, zoning, and how Oakland shaped American cannabis policy ."], [3, "2025 U.S. Industrial Real Estate: E-Commerce Logistics Demand ...\nJul 8, 2025 \u00b7 2025 industrial real estate outlook driven by e -commerce logistics expansion: warehouse demand by market, last-mile delivery trends, construction pipeline, and institutional investment opportunities."], [4, "E-commerce and logistics sprawl: A spatial exploration of ...\nOct 1, 2023 \u00b7 Do e -commerce logistics platforms open closer to urban consumers to meet heightened time- demands and do they decentralize in alignment with broader warehousing trends? This study employs centrographic techniques to measure platform distances from consumer centers and densities between 2013 and 2021."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["oakland", "oakland city council", "oakland redevelopment successor agency", "oakland city hall", "council president kevin jenkins", "janani ramachandran", "rowena brown", "noel gallo", "zac unger", "charlene wang", "carroll fife", "ken houston", "brown act", "measure z", "california department of cannabis control", "dcc", "california", "united states", "local zoning laws", "cannabis retail", "dispensaries", "american cannabis policy"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778701314.5049465, "duration_sec": 1.9543540477752686, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I also wrote a message to the whole company prior, about a month prior or something, just saying I was going to be taking off. I haven't done it before. And it was kind of an admission that this is a long time coming and just wanted to give everyone a head start on that. So I didn't just like drop off the map and be like, what happened? So I did that too. But that was, you know, that took 10 minutes to write."], [0, "And then, David, question for you. Did your role change with Jason being gone? Or was it just like, nope, I kept doing everything that I was always doing before? I don't think it was that different. I think Elaine and I would just meet on the weekly basis. Usually that's the three of us, and then it would just be the two of us. And it was the same sort of order of business. Now, part of the reason why this kind of worked was to accept that even though Jason was stepping away for six weeks here,"], [0, "there were certain things that were already in motion. Like, we weren't going to start a new product, for example. That wasn't going to happen while Jason was away. And it didn't need to happen. First of all, we'd already laid some foundations and there were some people working on that new product we're working on. So that could run in itself. And just realizing that there were certain kinds of projects that it didn't make sense to take on."], [0, "But there aren't that many. And as we talked about, they're quite infrequent, just like it probably, if I was, when I was out for six weeks, I'd probably be a little surprised if I came back and like, hey, by the way, we're off the cloud. Like we just, we set that up or Ruby, we're not using that anymore. We changed to JavaScript. That's what we program in now. So there are some of those sort of big foundational things"], [0, "tenets of how we build or how we work that you're not going to change when one of the principles is out for that amount of time. But those are also the kind of things where even if we wanted to change it, it wouldn't matter if that had to wait two months or even if it had to wait six months. Some of those big titanic switches are like once every few years kind of stuff. So if they have to wait a little further, it'd be just fine."], [0, "The other thing I want to add is one other change we made was to shift from exploration to production on some ideas we've been working on. So we're working on a new product right now. And we've been working on it for just a few months. And during those few months, we've just been exploring ideas."], [0, "And then I thought when I was gone, and I've been leading that charge. And so I thought when I was gone, it'd be a good idea to shift into like, let's build some of those ideas out. So when I come back, we have a basis of something that's actually working. And so that was also a nice change, a good shift and a good timing for that. But we needed to do that anyway. So it was just a matter of sequencing it at the right time. And I felt like that was a pretty good time to do that. So that was one other small change we made. But otherwise, everything was basically the same."], [0, "It's funny because we had something similar here with our cloud exit. We had to build some technology to facilitate how we were going to run our applications in the cloud. There's this thing called MERSC that I knew I had a two-month slot to focus all in where I had no distractions and no travel lined up at all."], [0, "And I knew there was a deadline coming up where I'd have about three weeks at least worth of travel. And then I would have a bit of a punctured schedule after that. So sometimes it's almost like you have the inverse. Do you know what? I'm going to take the inverse of a sabbatical right now. I'm going to ultra focus on this one thing for two months such that, as Jason said, you can get the project to the point of where it can go downhill."], [0, "We have this feature in Basecamp called the hill chart. And I really love the metaphor of the hill chart. The hill has two sides. It has the uphill side. That's when you're still figuring things out. You're not quite sure how the pieces are going to work together. You're not quite even sure how many pieces there are. You're still uncovering things. Then once you reach the top of the hill, that means you figure things out."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["sabbatical", "employee leave", "company announcement", "role changes", "weekly meetings", "project planning", "product development", "exploration", "production", "technology shifts", "cloud migration", "focus time", "engineering", "workplace communication", "scheduling", "software architecture", "project management", "basecamp"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778701991.1607003, "duration_sec": 2.020045518875122, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And the mental model that I have for this, interestingly enough, comes from when I was younger, where going on the internet was something that you had to kind of engage in as a explicit activity. In other words, you would log on to the internet rather than the internet just being kind of constantly available."], [0, "What this meant was we had these blocks of time, in our house at least, where we would take turns logging onto the internet, checking our email. And once that block of time was up, there was no such thing as a push notification that would interrupt dinner. If you can find a way to make your consumption habits explicit, then you'll choose what you consume more carefully."], [0, "And ultimately, you'll have the ability to explicitly, there's our key word for this episode, explicitly plan how you're going to spend the rest of that free time. Thank you so much for listening to today's episode of Developer Tea. Hopefully in the next couple of episodes, we're going to continue talking about these implicit and explicit systems, how we can take some of the implicit things and move them more towards the explicit systems."], [0, "and perhaps the other direction. How we can take some of the things that are explicit unnecessarily and taking up too much of our energy and move them more towards implicit."], [0, "Thank you so much for listening to today's episode of Developer Tea. We wouldn't be able to do what we do without our wonderful sponsors. Today's episode was sponsored by Git Prime. Go and check out the book that Git Prime has released. That's at gitprime.com slash 20patterns, gitprime.com slash 20patterns. Thanks again to Git Prime for sponsoring today's episode. Thank you so much for listening to today's episode. My name is Jonathan Cottrell. And until next time, enjoy your tea."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["internet usage", "email checking", "time blocks", "push notifications", "consumption habits", "explicit activity", "implicit systems", "explicit planning", "free time", "attention management", "habit formation", "productivity", "software development", "developer podcast", "sponsorship", "analytics", "digital wellbeing", "self control"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
