b7 Lee & Associates\u2019 2024 Q4 North America Market Report looks back at the tenant demand, absorption rates and vacancy trends for industrial, office , retail and multifamily sectors nationwide to ..."], [2, "United States Multifamily Capital Markets Report | Newmark\nNewmark presents the First Quarter 2025 United States Multifamily Capital Markets Report. The statistics and in-depth market perspective contained in the\u2026"], [3, "Mixed-Use Property Mortgage Guidelines - finhelp.io\nOct 20, 2025 \u00b7 Mixed-use property mortgage guidelines are the lender criteria used to evaluate properties that combine residential and commercial uses\u2014covering occupancy mix, income documentation, appraisal approach, down payment/LTV thresholds , credit standards, and lease verification to determine underwriting risk."], [4, "DSCR Loans for Commercial Mixed-Use Properties: Retail ...\nDSCR financing for mixed-use properties: retail + residential, office + residential qualification, rental income calculation for mixed-use , zoning requirements , commercial occupancy thresholds , 5+ unit transition, and wholesale broker access to mixed-use DSCR programs. NMLS #1426884."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": null}, "prompt_chain": null, "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["commercial real estate", "multifamily housing", "office space", "retail property", "industrial property", "mixed use property", "market report", "net absorption", "vacancy rates", "real estate", "property investment", "housing development", "apartment construction", "mortgage rates", "loan underwriting", "dscr loans", "zoning reform", "permitting process", "housing policy", "capital markets", "north america"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778702676.1735537, "duration_sec": 3.0526907444000244, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "They really\u2014 they really only come when they have to."], [1, "Yeah."], [3, "The reason they don't like it is because of the immigration, the way they treat them when they're crossing. They always get harassed. Yeah. When they're going back to America, they get harassed. So they get tired of that."], [1, "Los Algodones has long been set up for the benefit of Americans, and the journey Americans take to cross into Molar City has always been a simple one. But of course, the journey in the opposite direction is much more complicated. Can I ask you a question that might sound\u2014 I don't know, I don't know how you'll take it, but I'll ask it anyway. You can tell me how you react to it. I've heard from Americans and Canadians, like people describing what it's like to go down to Los Algodones, and people describe just walking freely through the border, like just 5 minutes, no need to even show a passport. And I, I'm just curious what that's like for you on your end, knowing that you were kicked out, and yet part of your job is to interact daily with I don't know, hundreds of people who just walk so freely across this border, you know?"], [3, "The way I see it is like this. Since I am from the border and we lived in America, we went to school in America, what happens is that when I see people coming from America and I see myself that I can't go back, which is like literally Right there, I can see my mom's house from right here."], [1, "Oh, wow."], [3, "That's the hardest thing to see. I see my house. I got the Colorado River, and I can literally see the water tank at the reservation, and then I can see all the way to the Yuma hospital on 24th Street, and then I kind of know more or less where my mom's house is over here. If I had a drone, I could fly it over there and and look at and go, \"Ouch.\"."], [1, "Yeah."], [3, "That's how close I am. That's not easy. It's very hard for me."], [1, "It's 5:00 AM in Algodones. The sky is still dark, not quite sunrise, and Alberto is shuffling around the house, getting ready for for work. The streets are mostly quiet at this hour, just the sound of other promoters and vendors setting up, setting the stage for Molar City. By 6:00 AM, he's at his post. The border opens and the Americans descend. They walk in the shadow of the 30-foot wall that divides them. Crossing the very threshold Alberto cannot."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["immigration", "border crossing", "harassment", "deportation", "mexican american border", "los algodones", "americans", "inequality", "family separation", "daily work", "travel restrictions", "emotional hardship", "indigenous reservation", "colorado river", "morning routine", "wall barrier", "human rights", "social justice"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778702867.3468235, "duration_sec": 1.9545233249664307, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Even like telling that story because it sounds like bullshit. It really happened that way because then the guard\u2014 I talked to the guard. I'm like, hey, when did the lights go out? Oh, it shook and the lights went out. I'm like, I'm watching that happen."], [1, "So you felt it happen before it happened."], [0, "Some kind of weird way."], [1, "Well, I bet humans have that. Animals definitely have that. They talk about Thailand. How they had that tsunami and all the animals ran up to the highest point of the island. They all just took off. It was like they just knew instinctively."], [0, "I don't know. Nothing like that has ever happened afterwards. But I gotta say, there's been\u2014 I've been lucky over the years."], [1, "Yeah, but you're a dude who's tuned in. You're tuned into your body. You're tuned into your environment. You're not gonna get caught slipping. Like, you probably felt something and your spidey sense went off."], [0, "Yeah, I kind of have been like that growing up. Like, I've been, you know, I've been on my own since I was 14. Been through crazy shit that you normally would see on movies."], [1, "And that's the type of shit that gives you those kind of instincts."], [0, "But yeah, and I was always the one that said, \"Hey, let's leave. Let's get out of here.\" And then, \"Hey, man, there was a shootout that just happened right after you left.\" Or I could detect, like, The predators, you know what I mean?"], [1, "Mm-hmm."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_c