{"timestamp": 1778702902.1889365, "duration_sec": 2.1209466457366943, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Okay, it's Liam."], [1, "What's crazy is these characters are like more expensive than Space Marine characters by a reasonably significant margin."], [0, "Oh yeah, yeah, the price has gone up on everything. I mean, even, even the units are starting to get a little overpriced, uh, but we've got the speed, man. It's a really, really fast army."], [1, "Bunch of Hawks. Yeah, this looks pretty standard. It's got a unit of Shining Spears."], [0, "Interesting. Yeah, I think this is exactly Liam's kind of current variation, which you can see at Fireside Discord. Liam's been sharing and chatting about it as well. So, hey, my man."], [1, "Yeah, we have, uh, Max, uh, I'm not gonna pronounce that last name because I'm from the southern hemisphere and I'll butcher that."], [0, "And we've got\u2014 this is Guardian Battlehost. Wow, okay, so that's the two different archetypes really. You can get the, uh, the MSU Aspect Host, or you can get the, the chunky bricks Guardian Battlehost. Actually, Uriel fits in quite nicely in Guardian Battlehost with the redeploy. They have an infiltrate for their Guardian bricks. Yeah, so you can end up moving them about. Just a solid\u2014 oh, I don't know about Wraithlords. It was all good until you got to the Wraithlords."], [1, "JHB could do some serious damage, man. But I don't see any of the."], [0, "D-cannons. I think they're unfortunately just too expensive now because, I mean, Storm Guardians went from 100 to 120, Eldrad went up an extra chunk, so every list got hit by the points increases."], [1, "Nice, that was awesome. So yeah, that's Pyro Cup. I'm playing at Manchester this weekend coming up, and, uh, yeah, everything's good. Vic is now a CSM player, so I'm expecting the CSM chat to be getting lit up there in a minute."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Gaming"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["warhammer", "army list", "points increase", "miniature pricing", "space marines", "aeldari", "shining spears", "swooping hawks", "guardian battlehost", "aspect host", "wraithlords", "d cannons", "tabletop gaming", "tournament play", "discord community", "competitive strategy", "manchester event", "chaos space marines"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703572.8123412, "duration_sec": 3.1688730716705322, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So in this case, it was just that we did much more aggressive caching in our builds. So here it was really just seeing whether the total runtime was less than what we had before. But that was very noticeable because before it was like 26 minutes. And after doing some very aggressive caching, we got down to six minutes."], [0, "Okay. So what runs the CI CD? Is it GitHub Actions? It's GitHub Actions, yeah. Okay. We just do like, so previously we did, we did most of our caching through like Docker layers, but we ran into a couple of issues with that where"], [0, "I don't remember exactly anymore what the problem was, but there were some permission issues and we couldn't figure out why that was happening. And the saving and loading of Docker caches was actually taking longer than running the builds. And so we decided we're not going to do the actual build within the Docker files anymore. Because we have 100% statically linked Go binaries, that's all that Polar Signals Cloud is made up of."], [0, "So we're building the statically linked binaries before, and then we just put those into containers. And so basically, all we're doing is we're using the Go caching from GitHub Actions now."], [0, "I see, I see. Okay, so it's, I think you're thinking about the BuildKit caching. So the BuildKit caching integration with the GitHub Actions cache is slower than actually running the commands. And I have seen this before, and there's like a great story for another time behind that. And Eric is someone that I work with, and he's one of the BuildKit core maintainers. And, you know, he's well aware of this, and he's working towards a solution."], [0, "But I know what you mean. I know that sometimes using the layer caching, the built layer caching with GitHub Actions can be slower for sure. Okay, that makes sense. So where do you build those binaries, the statically linked Go binaries? Those we build just through normal GitHub Actions. And beforehand, we load the GoMod cache from previous runs, and then we save the cache if it changes."], [0, "Okay, yeah, that makes sense. Okay, I can see that. So in six minutes, you get your change out in the staging cluster. And then what happens afterwards? I mean, then people review it. The cool thing is because we also run like previews on Vercel. Basically, you can try out the entire pull request."], [0, "after six minutes. We've got the UIs that can either be pointed at different versions of the API or even the production API because most of the time it's either or, like a pull request that only does changes to the front end."], [0, "And in that case, it's actually nicer if you can just use production data immediately. So, yeah. And then if it's approved and merged, then within the next six minutes, it's going to be deployed. Nice. How many deploys do you do per day? Because this sounds very efficient. You must be doing a lot."], [0, "Yeah, I mean, it depends on what people are working on, but we can easily do tens of deploys if we want to. That's very nice. That makes a huge difference. Being able to make small changes, try them out in the final place where they will run, gaining that confidence, and then just saying, yep, this looks good to me. And then a few minutes later, in this case, several minutes later, you have it. Nice."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["ci cd", "github actions", "build caching", "docker layers", "buildkit", "go binaries", "static linking", "build time", "cache performance", "staging deployment", "vercel previews", "pull requests", "devops", "containerization", "software engineering", "continuous delivery", "permissions", "code review"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
