{"timestamp": 1778708549.839656, "duration_sec": 2.164625406265259, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Hello, welcome to another episode of Rustation Station. I'm your host, Alan Wyma. Today we have Gabor. I hope I said that correctly. Yeah, quite close. Gabor. He's the creator of Rust Digger. Welcome, Gabor. How are you?"], [0, "Thank you very much. I'm quite fine and happy to be here. So Russ Digger, I mean, the name itself doesn't really explain what it is. Maybe you can give a quick synopsis of what it is."], [0, "This is the force of these digger sites that I created. And the idea is to dig in, dig out or dig in or whatever in English. Interesting things about, in the case of Rust, of crates. But I created a similar site for Perl, one for Python and one for Ruby as well, for the respective third-party libraries. So sort of trying to..."], [0, "dig out interesting things from there. That was the idea of the name. I'm not really sure how it sounds in English, so whatever. Well, now that you use the word dig, right, it starts to make a little bit more sense why it's called RustDigger. I mean, I've never heard this name before. So then your other sites that you've created, like for Perl and Python, they're also called PythonDigger and et cetera, right?"], [0, "Not exactly. For Perl, it's called Cpandigger. The Ruby, I think it's RubyDigger. For Python, it's PyDigger. They all are slightly different and at slightly different stages of development and usage."], [0, "The whole idea is basically to try to find a couple of things. One of them is to try to analyze the common practices. So we know this title of best practices in software development or packaging or whatever. And I noticed that there are many times several best practices that are even contradicting."], [0, "But my idea was to try to find the common practices, let's say if you're talking about Rust, of the crates, of how crates are being created, what is being used. And looking at Rust and crates, it's very standardized. It's way more standardized than the other three languages. So in a way, there's a lot less to analyze in that side because we don't have"], [0, "I mean, there are not, not that I know at least, several packaging systems and several ways of testing. There are not that many different approaches, either because the standard is so well thought out or because there just haven't been enough time to create all kinds of different approaches."], [0, "But that's sort of the basic idea, that was sort of the basic idea to try to find the, analyze these packages and then to start to recommend to the package managers or crate maintainers various things. The simplest that I suggest actually is that each crate has,"], [0, "has an optional field that tells you where is the repository of that crate, where is the version control."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["rust", "crates", "package analysis", "software development", "best practices", "common practices", "rustdigger", "perl", "python", "ruby", "third party libraries", "package management", "version control", "open source", "tooling", "repository metadata", "language ecosystems", "standardization", "data mining"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778708582.8388987, "duration_sec": 2.0139124393463135, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "But there's apples were bad for Adam."], [1, "I don't even know if it was an apple."], [0, "It was a fruit."], [1, "It was a fruit from the tree of knowledge, the tree of\u2014 it was an apple. It was an apple, but it wasn't an apple tree. What specifically does the Bible refer to as the fruit?"], [0, "Adam and Eve. Adam, you know the truth."], [1, "What? Eve never talked to God. Adam talked to God. Adam told God not to eat the fruit. There's nowhere in the Bible does it say that Adam went and told Eve."], [0, "This is why we should start not just shutting women down to listening to them."], [1, "It all started\u2014 Genesis does not specifically specify, rather, what kind of fruit Adam ate, only that it calls it fruit from the tree of knowledge of good and evil. Yeah, so it's not necessarily an apple. We call it an apple. Uh, the text never names the species\u2014 apple, fig, etc. The Hebrew word is peri, a general term meaning fruit without a botanical detail. Where the apple idea came from\u2014 over time, Jewish and Christian interpreters proposed many candidates, including fig, grape, pomegranate, citron, and others. Now, common ideas that an apple developed later in European tradition, helped by wordplay in Latin and Old French, where the words evil and apple or fruit sounded or were spelled similarly. So it's not necessarily an apple."], [0, "I really don't know what to believe. I feel like I get so much more information when I come here. Yeah, I don't know if people understand that."], [1, "Well, the crazy thing is that, I mean, I found this out recently because I've actually been reading the Bible, that there's no reference whatsoever to Adam telling Eve you're not supposed to eat the fruit from the plant with the knowledge of good and evil."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["bible", "genesis", "adam and eve", "forbidden fruit", "tree of knowledge", "good and evil", "apple", "christian tradition", "jewish interpretation", "biblical text", "hebrew language", "gender roles", "latin language", "old french", "linguistics", "translation", "religious symbolism", "decision making"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778708689.856753, "duration_sec": 1.8018949031829834, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "quote, high performers at 37 Signals. I imagine you think about this quite differently than the rest of the tech industry. And that comes from Keith. David, you want to jump in on that? Yes. First of all, high performance have multiple dimensions in it. One of the dimensions, as he mentions, is skill. Are you good at what you do? That's really important. You're not going to get beyond a senior programmer or senior designer at 37 Signals unless you're really good at what you do."], [0, "But to go beyond that, to become a lead programmer or a principal programmer or lead designer or principal designer or something else, there is more than just individual skill to be part of it. There is this notion we often called showing up. How do you show up? Do you run to problems? Are you the kind of person that others want to go to because they know you're just going to address it, that you're very efficient?"], [0, "Those are all factors that are codified in our progression ladders of seniority. What is the difference between a senior and a lead? What's the difference between a lead and a principal? And one of the things I pride myself on is that we recognize, broadly speaking, that there are no speed limits."], [0, "If you're able to come into the organization and maybe you're hired as a senior programmer, how long does it take for you to be able to demonstrate lead qualities? That's less about calendar time and far more about how you choose to engage with the work and how you choose to run to problems, how you choose to take on more responsibility for a whole subsystem or for a whole product or for your team's ability to be able to deliver."], [0, "All of those things are exactly in that category that Jason referred to originally of his definition of work ethic. They're not about more hours. They're not about just putting in 10 or 12 hours. They're about doing what you say you're going to do, finding the problems before someone else needs to report it, running to those problems that"], [0, "delivering on what you said you would deliver. It's not about fulfilling someone else's assessment of how long something should take. It's about your own responsibility to your own statements. So absolutely, there's a huge difference in those things. And your personal capacity as a programmer, designer, marketing person, or whatever, it's just one track. And there are multiple tracks that..."], [0, "go together to form career progression at 37 singles. And especially when you get up to the sort of more senior levels, you will tap out on skill alone. You will tap out on skill alone at our company at senior. That's literally codified in the handbook. You can link to them in the show notes. We have all these different levels described. What does it mean to be the different things? And you'll see, you'll tap out. You can be the best program in the world."], [0, "I don't know, maybe the best program in the world could be something else, but you can be a very good programmer and senior is still sort of the level you will rest at here at the company, unless there's some of these other factors at present too."], [0, "The other thing I want to add is my guess is I feel like he wants to ask this question, but he's not quite getting to it, which is like, do bonus people who are performing better than others? I just feel like maybe that's hidden in the question somewhere. No. So like the question is, he's like, how do you reward high performers? When you think about rewards, typically in business, it's like financial rewards is kind of how it is. But, you know, different people, first of all, everything David said is correct, which is like,"], [0, "If you did really well on this project, well, that's something in the win column for you. It doesn't mean like you get a promotion. It doesn't mean you get a bonus. It doesn't mean any of those things. It's like you have to pile a bunch of these things up that show that over the long term or there's a trend here that you're possibly able to move up a level in the company based on a number of different criteria."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["high performance", "tech industry", "skill development", "career progression", "seniority levels", "leadership roles", "work ethic", "responsibility", "problem solving", "team delivery", "promotions", "financial rewards", "software engineering", "organizational culture", "employee motivation", "human resources", "performance evaluation", "decision making"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778709205.5055614, "duration_sec": 12.685854434967041, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Seattle Council Committee Report\nSelect Committee on the Library Levy\nCouncil Chamber, City Hall\nWednesday, April 8, 2026, 9:30 AM\n600 4th Avenue\nSeattle, WA 98104\nThis meeting also constitutes a meeting of the City Council, provided that the meeting shall be conducted as a\ncommittee meeting under the Council Rules and Procedures, and Council action shall be limited to committee\nbusiness.\nPlease Note: Times listed are estimated\nA. Call To Order\nMeeting Start Time: 9:34 a.m.\nPresiding Officer: Councilmember Rivera\nPresent: 7 - Maritza Rivera, Joy Hollingsworth, Dionne Foster, Debora Juarez, Eddie Lin, Alexis\nMercedes Rinck,Dan Strauss\nExcused: 2 - Robert Kettle,Rob Saka\nB. Approval of the Agenda\nC. Public Comment\nD. Items of Business\n1. CB 121181 AN ORDINANCE relating to regular property taxes; providing for the\nsubmission to the qualified electors of the City at an election to be held on\nAugust 4, 2026, of a proposition authorizing the City to levy regular property\ntaxes for up to seve"], [0, "Greenlake Branch Seismic Retrofit Project\nThe Seattle Public Library Green Lake Branch, one of the Library \u2019s historic Carnegie buildings , will be renovated with structural improvements (a seismic retrofit ) to ensure the safety of patrons and staff in case of an earthquake ."], [1, "URM Seismic Upgrade and Renovation Seattle Public Library ...\nThe Project is a structural upgrade and renovation of an existing 2-story concrete and unreinforced masonry building including renovated staff areas, seismic upgrades , and accessibility improvements including a new elevator."], [2, "Zoning - SDCI | seattle.gov\nOur Zoning Code determines how land is used and developed in Seattle . Zoning districts are adopted by ordinance and specify the types of uses allowed in each district."], [3, "Seattle Atlas \u2013 Seattle Zoning Map\nSearch Seattle zoning by neighborhood, explore what can be built where, and see transit and proposed changes side by side. Seattle Atlas brings zoning , land use , transit, and development proposals into a single interactive map."], [4, "Land Use - SDCI | seattle.gov\nLand Use division staff process and issue land use permits, also called master use permits, and conduct specialized reviews in land use , zoning , and environmental regulations on proposed development."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "select committee on the library levy", "seattle public library", "green lake branch", "green lake", "green lake branch seismic retrofit project", "urm seismic upgrade and renovation", "seattle department of construction and inspections", "seattle zoning code", "seattle zoning map", "seattle atlas", "cb 121181", "councilmember mar