areas, seismic upgrades , and accessibility improvements including a new elevator."], [2, "Zoning - SDCI | seattle.gov\nOur Zoning Code determines how land is used and developed in Seattle . Zoning districts are adopted by ordinance and specify the types of uses allowed in each district."], [3, "Seattle Atlas \u2013 Seattle Zoning Map\nSearch Seattle zoning by neighborhood, explore what can be built where, and see transit and proposed changes side by side. Seattle Atlas brings zoning , land use , transit, and development proposals into a single interactive map."], [4, "Land Use - SDCI | seattle.gov\nLand Use division staff process and issue land use permits, also called master use permits, and conduct specialized reviews in land use , zoning , and environmental regulations on proposed development."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "select committee on the library levy", "seattle city hall", "councilmember maritza rivera", "councilmember joy hollingsworth", "councilmember dionne foster", "councilmember debora juarez", "councilmember eddie lin", "councilmember alexis mercedes rinck", "councilmember dan strauss", "councilmember robert kettle", "councilmember rob saka", "council bill cb 121181", "library levy", "seattle public library", "green lake branch", "green lake branch seismic retrofit project", "urm seismic upgrade and renovation", "seattle department of construction and inspections", "seattle zoning code"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778709281.6325252, "duration_sec": 1.9684410095214844, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Greetings and welcome to the Take One Security Podcast. My name is Daniel Meisler and this podcast is me reading and commenting on InfoSec headlines all in one take. There will be no preparation, no editing, very little post-production. Goal is to simply give people a way to audibly consume security news with a bit of analysis in less than 15 minutes. Today is March 3rd, 2015, and let's get started. So there's a new SSL attack called Freak."], [0, "It basically has to do with RSA falling back to a deprecated and weak encryption strength. The solution is basically to patch. You have to patch both client if you're using a vulnerable browser, and of course, OpenSSL as well. A number of orgs will probably want to note which servers actually have vulnerable versions to"], [0, "trying to figure out if any data could have been compromised. Although Jarrett, Mike Grossman had a great point on Twitter a few minutes ago, basically saying, is there any evidence that this is any SSL attack has been used not by a nation state or a major corporation? In other words, is this really a sort of a practical attack? I think it's an interesting question"], [0, "Although I think the Anthem thing could have been leaked or linked to the Heartbleed stuff, but I'm not sure if that's true. I'm not sure if that was followed up on. Interesting stuff though. The thing about this is that this insecurity was built into SSL as a security feature."], [0, "And I guess that's the one takeaway is don't add insecurity in order to gain security. And in formula form, that might look like backdoor times time equals regret. So it doesn't seem to make any sense. And hopefully, we'll learn from this mistake, although I doubt it. So next story, using Ruby's open URI could be dangerous."], [0, "Basically, the open URI option uses monkey patches inside of kernel.open. So you're basically doing a kernel patch when you use this functionality in Ruby. You basically are opening params, passing a URL, and you can actually open things like pipe ls and actually run system commands."], [0, "There's some discussion about how it's somewhat safe to do this, and the alternative is not much better. But definitely something to understand if you're parsing in content from unknown locations. And one way to think about that is, oh, you mean I need to do input validation? Well, I thought I already knew that. But I don't think many people know that you can pass system commands into OpenURI and have them execute and return results."], [0, "So if you're parsing or consuming some list of URIs or URLs from some location, and you could have malicious content in there, you need to be careful if you're passing it to open URI. Hillary Clinton used her personal email address and did not store correspondence on government servers for her entire four years as Secretary of Defense."], [0, "This is pretty scary. First of all, you're putting the data at risk by having it in some random personal system. And second, it seems pretty obvious that you're trying to hide your conversation. So I don't know how this is even legal or how you just file for an exception for that. Seems highly suspect to me, and it's quite interesting that it happened. Seems like this is the type of thing you would specifically try to avoid"], [0, "if you're trying to put on some sort of air of transparency. That was something that Obama talked about is transparency. How do you hire a secretary of state and not require that her email is visible to citizens? Pretty interesting. So Facebook can access your account without your password. This is good to know. Evidently, any Facebook employee"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["ssl vulnerabilities", "freak attack", "rsa encryption", "openssl patching", "security updates", "data compromise", "heartbleed", "ruby programming", "openuri", "command injection", "input validation", "government email", "nation state threats", "cybersecurity journalism", "software engineering", "privacy transparency", "social media security", "security backdoors"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778709784.8759794, "duration_sec": 2.1336276531219482, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "But there's apples were bad for Adam."], [1, "I don't even know if it was an apple."], [0, "It was a fruit."], [1, "It was a fruit from the tree of knowledge, the tree of\u2014 it was an apple. It was an apple, but it wasn't an apple tree. What specifically does the Bible refer to as the fruit?"], [0, "Adam and Eve. Adam, you know the truth."], [1, "What? Eve never talked to God. Adam talked to God. Adam told God not to eat the fruit. There's nowhere in the Bible does it say that Adam went and told Eve."], [0, "This is why we should start not just shutting women down to listening to them."], [1, "It all started\u2014 Genesis does not specifically specify, rather, what kind of fruit Adam ate, only that it calls it fruit from the tree of knowledge of good and evil. Yeah, so it's not necessarily an apple. We call it an apple. Uh, the text never names the species\u2014 apple, fig, etc. The Hebrew word is peri, a general term meaning fruit without a botanical detail. Where the apple idea came from\u2014 over time, Jewish and Christian interpreters proposed many candidates, including fig, grape, pomegranate, citron, and others. Now, common ideas that an apple developed later in European tradition, helped by wordplay in Latin and Old French, where the words evil and apple or fruit sounded or were spelled similarly. So it's not necessarily an apple."], [0, "I really don't know what to believe. I feel like I get so much more information when I come here. Yeah, I don't know if people understand that."], [1, "Well, the crazy thing is that, I mean, I found this out recently because I've actually been reading the Bible, that there's no reference whatsoever to Adam telling Eve you're not supposed to eat the fruit from the plant with the knowledge of good and evil."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["bible", "genesis", "adam and eve", "forbidden fruit", "tree of knowledge", "good and evil", "apple", "biblical interpretation", "scripture reading", "hebrew language", "jewish tradition", "christian tradition", "latin", "european folklore", "translation", "mythology", "gender roles", "critical thinking"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
