{"timestamp": 1778711026.2895672, "duration_sec": 2.3431644439697266, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And I think that's probably the most honest thing anyone around this case has said, because that's the question that would eat you alive. Not how could he do it, but how could I not know? How did I sleep in that house? How did I raise my children there? How did I miss every single sign for three decades? And maybe the answer is that there were no signs. Not for her. Because Rex Heurman, by his own account..."], [0, "To his daughter, Victoria kept those words completely separate. Victoria Heerman sat across from the same man and asked the question that cuts deepest. She asked her father whether he ever stopped to think that the women he killed were somebody's daughter, somebody's family member, somebody who was loved. And according to Victoria, he said he didn't see them as human."], [0, "She pushed back further. He told her his demons got to him. He described urges that surfaced when certain opportunities or catalysts presented themselves. He said he kept his family and his victims in completely separate worlds, two realities that never touched each other in his mind. He told his daughter that the woman he killed, the women that he killed, weren't people to him. They weren't real, not the way his family was real."], [0, "Victoria's struggle with this on camera is gutting. She says it plainly. He was a loving dad and a serial killer and she can't make sense of those two things in the same person. She says it's hard to stomach that even that seven women lost their lives in the house where she grew up. She talks about being just mere feet away from where she would play video games, where she would have fun childhood memories of"], [0, "hanging out in the basement, sometimes with dad. How do you square those things up? Victoria didn't choose any of this. She was born into it. And the timeline of her birth is where the story also reaches a detail that's also very hard to sit with. One of the killings Rex Hiramann confessed to happened days before his destination wedding to Asa. Karen Vergata, the eighth victim, the uncharged one,"], [0, "In February of 1996, Asa flew to Europe to prepare for their wedding. Rex stayed behind on Long Island for several days. During that window, Karen Vergata disappeared. She was last seen on February 14th. Her partial remains were found weeks later on Fire Island. More of her remains turned up near Gilgo Beach in 2011. 96 is when she went missing. 2011."], [0, "It's almost 20 years after. According to prosecutors, Rex Ehrman allegedly took care and forgot his life during those days. Asa was overseas. Then he flew to Europe to get married to Asa Elrup. And months later, their daughter Victoria was born."], [0, "This final episode captures Asa confronting Hiraman about this specific timeline. She's asking him to walk her through the planning of a murder that fell in the days before their wedding, months before the birth of their child. She already knew the numbers. She already knew the timelines, but she still asked him to lay it out. And I think that's significant, not because it proves some hidden motive, but because it tells you what Asa is actually doing with these visits."], [0, "She's trying to map the life she thought she had against the life that was actually happening. She's trying to understand how the man who flew to Europe to marry her is the same man who allegedly killed a woman days before the flight. And she can't. Nobody could."], [0, "But she keeps going back because somewhere in the gap between those two realities is the explanation for her own life. And she hasn't found it yet. Maybe she never will. So what do you do when you can't reconcile your life with what you know it actually was?"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["serial killer", "family trauma", "murder confession", "victim dehumanization", "psychological separation", "long island", "gilgo beach", "fire island", "wedding timeline", "missing persons", "criminal investigation", "documentary episode", "domestic life", "forensic evidence", "mental health", "grief processing", "media storytelling", "moral accountability"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778711694.8630743, "duration_sec": 4.949044466018677, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Seattle Council Committee Report\nLand Use and Sustainability Committee\nCouncil Chamber, City Hall\nWednesday, April 1, 2026, 9:30 AM\n600 4th Avenue\nSeattle, WA 98104\nThis meeting also constitutes a meeting of the City Council, provided that the meeting shall be conducted as a\ncommittee meeting under the Council Rules and Procedures, and Council action shall be limited to committee\nbusiness.\nPlease Note: Times listed are estimated\nA. Call To Order\nMeeting Start Time: 9:37 a.m.\nPresiding Officer: Councilmember Lin\nPresent: 5 - Eddie Lin, Dan Strauss, Dionne Foster, Joy Hollingsworth,Alexis Mercedes Rinck\nB. Approval of the Agenda\nC. Public Comment\nD. Items of Business\n1. Appt 03463 Appointment of Phoebe Erin Bogert as member, Seattle Design\nCommission, for a term to February 28, 2027.\nThe Committee recommends that City Council confirm the Appointment (Appt).\nIn Favor: 5 - Lin, Strauss, Foster, Hollingsworth,Rinck\nOpposed: None\n2. Appt 03464 Appointment of Tatum Lau as member, Seattle Desi"], [0, "NYC Zoning Updates: \u2018City of Yes\u2019 Reshapes Housing ...\nJan 27, 2025 \u00b7 In December 2024, the New York City Council approved \u201cCity of Yes for Housing Opportunity,\u201d an amendment to the Zoning Resolution to address New York City\u2019s housing crisis by updating zoning rules to allow for incremental additional housing in every neighborhood."], [1, "Zoning Overlays: A Powerful Tool for Growth and Revitalization\nOct 29, 2025 \u00b7 Zoning overlay districts are land use regulations that sit on top of an existing zone, allowing municipalities to respond to changing community needs, like climate change and economic revitalization, without overhauling their entire zoning framework."], [2, "Adaptive Reuse Tax Credits: Complete Incentives Guide\nFeb 6, 2026 \u00b7 Learn how adaptive reuse tax credits maximize project returns. Covers federal HTC, NMTC, Opportunity Zones, state programs, and credit stacking strategies."], [3, "Revitalizing Downtowns Act: Incentives and Eligibility\nDec 14, 2025 \u00b7 Navigate the Revitalizing Downtowns Act framework. Learn eligibility, financial incentives , application procedures, and long-term compliance."], [4, "New Bill Proposes Incentives for Adaptive Reuse\nApr 30, 2025 \u00b7 The National Apartment Association (NAA) supports adaptive reuse policies that help housing providers bridge the financial gap to make redevelopment possible. Federal incentives , like this proposal, help increase the speed in which housing developers are able to help transform communities."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "land use and sustainability committee", "seattle council committee report", "seattle city hall", 